comments[16]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-06T23:51:07.660Z",codex-agent,"Planning evidence 2026-07-07: all-status duplicate checks showed pm-4k6b is the canonical adaptive context engine parent; no focused child existed for signal extraction, scorer contract, or token-budget packing. Added non-duplicate implementation slices: pm-3hps (derived signal feature store), pm-h3no (pluggable SDK scorer contract), and pm-55ra (diversity-aware token-budget packer). Algorithm sketch: build an index-backed candidate set from focus/claim/query/dependency windows; compute normalized signal vectors for recency, activity density, graph proximity, priority/risk/deadline pressure, semantic similarity, knowledge density, author affinity, and continuity deltas; combine them through a deterministic default scorer with extension override; pack by marginal utility per token while preserving required anchors and degrading projection depth before dropping items; expose score breakdowns for explainability and pm-atfm evaluation.\n"
  "2026-07-09T18:58:16.013Z",codex-root-20260709,"Implementation bundle: activating the canonical parent because pm-h3no, pm-atfm, pm-lixy, and pm-xmp5 deliver the first SDK-first adaptive relevance tranche across scorer contracts, context/next integration, golden judgments, and regression evaluation. Blocked index-backed signal store pm-3hps and token packer pm-55ra remain dependency-gated and are not falsely claimed."
  "2026-07-09T19:10:26.630Z",codex-root-20260709,"Progress evidence: wave-one adaptive relevance delivers the public scorer contract, deterministic weighted default, extension replacement path, context/next integration, explanations, and quality harness while preserving dependency gates for index-backed signals and token packing."
  "2026-07-09T19:50:43.767Z",codex-root-20260709,"Bundle evidence: default weighted scorer, governed context_relevance extension override, shared context/next metadata signals, opt-in low-token explanations, public evaluation primitives, golden corpus, CI regression gate, docs, and installed-package proof are complete for this wave. Parent remains in progress for later semantic/index/packer waves."
  "2026-07-09T23:53:30.816Z",codex-root-20260709,"Handoff after PR #487: the first broad adaptive-context relevance primitives are merged with SDK scorer/evaluator contracts, explainable CLI output, regression gates, docs, and exact coverage. Parent remains in_progress for the explicitly linked corpus-scale and downstream relevance roadmap."
  "2026-07-10T00:03:03.300Z",codex-root-20260709,"Post-merge Gemini review follow-up: harden the public scorer against unknown JavaScript override keys and aggregate only canonical evaluation metrics. Review threads: discussion_r3555533540 and discussion_r3555533546."
  "2026-07-10T00:16:50.692Z",codex-root-20260709,"Review-fix evidence: unknown JavaScript weight keys are ignored, evaluator aggregation is pinned to the exported canonical metric tuple, and all required SDK thresholds must be finite. Focused 15 tests, typecheck, lint/static/docstring/token gates, full 5,502-test suite, exact 100/100/100/100 coverage, and the four-scenario corpus gate all pass."
  "2026-07-10T05:53:28.764Z",codex-root-20260709,"Verification evidence 2026-07-10: full sandbox suite passed 270 files / 5,505 tests with exact 100% statements, branches, functions, and lines. pm test-all over the seven active items passed 8 linked commands with 0 failures and 1 duplicate-safe skip. pnpm typecheck, contracts:check, lint (ESLint + 0 jscpd clones + static quality/token budget), docs-skills, context-eval, security scan, and audit all passed. Live CodeFactor reports A+ and 0 issues. Fresh packed npm install at /tmp/pm-cli-sdk-path-proof-XIsSsh activated 11 bundled packages in a path-target tracker, left caller extension files at zero, and passed CLI/SDK context plus isolated doctor with zero blocking failures. pm health is clean and history drift is zero."
  "2026-07-13T21:41:31.888Z",codex-root,"Context-engine review 2026-07-13: the existing algorithm ledger already covers retrieval decay, graph activation, learned ranking and fusion, diversity-aware token packing, incremental folds and sketches, adaptive granularity, analogical fingerprints, open-loop tension, co-change and outcome projections, salience, contradiction handling, gist compaction, successor representation, intent profiles, anytime assembly, temporal change points, empirical-Bayes uncertainty, and lineage continuity. Avoid duplicate speculative algorithm items. pm-55ra and pm-uwfs are shipped; the current execution sequence is pm-801d then pm-3hps, with pm-atfm and pm-lixy carrying evaluation and quality work."
  "2026-07-18T14:08:37.656Z",codex-ecosystem-planner,"Algorithm catalog verification 2026-07-18: all-status search plus full notes/history review confirms the 22-part context-engine ledger is canonical and already covers scale-free activation decay, typed spreading activation, explainable linear learning-to-rank, reciprocal-rank fusion, bounded multi-source recall, incremental event folds, sketch-backed statistics, submodular marginal-utility packing, granularity-adaptive representation choice, structural analogical fingerprints, open-loop tension floors, co-change/outcome projection, salience/consolidation, contradiction handling, temporal change points, uncertainty calibration, continuity, anytime assembly, and derived memory tiers. No speculative duplicate item was created. Execution substrate remains pm-801d -> pm-3hps; pm-atfm + pm-lixy own graded judgments, scale scenarios, calibration, and regression proof; pm-55ra/pm-h3no/pm-uwfs are shipped foundations. Acceptance should optimize constrained expected context utility: maximize judged task relevance + actionability + diversity + continuity minus contradiction risk and token cost, subject to hard inclusion of owned open loops, deterministic bounded latency, provenance/explanations, and small-project parity."
  "2026-07-22T05:00:36.947Z",codex-root-20260722,"Implementation start 2026-07-22: actively deliver the shared index-backed adaptive-context acceptance surface across pm-4k6b, pm-3hps, and pm-801d. Scope is CLI/SDK context and next integration, continuously maintained derived signals, deterministic explainability, bounded token packing, and scan-fallback parity. All-status duplicate review reused this canonical three-item lineage; no parallel context item was created."
  "2026-07-22T05:15:04.495Z",codex-root-20260722,"Progress evidence 2026-07-22: context and next now consume the shared cursor-bound SDK signal store, expose feature-store provenance only on explained output, accept explicit positive token budgets across CLI/SDK/MCP contracts, and retain separate projection namespaces. Focused compiler, lint, contract, and TDD checks are underway before full coverage."
  "2026-07-22T05:38:56.897Z",codex-root-20260722,"Verification evidence 2026-07-22: shared context/next ranking and packing pass exact 100/100/100/100 coverage and the five-scenario relevance gate at perfect NDCG, reciprocal rank, required recall, continuity coverage, and token adherence. A fresh 10k scale run measured context p95 583ms CLI and 207ms SDK with 1,300/892 estimated tokens, inside product targets. The overall scale gate remains red only for the already-reproduced unrelated get token baselines (CLI 1,042>322; SDK 701>288), so the rolling 100k/1M parent remains open."
  "2026-07-22T05:38:58.139Z",codex-root-20260722,"Release-gate note: every local release stage through build, typecheck, docs, strict static/docstring quality, exact coverage, version policy, secret scan, packed smoke, package-first dogfood, compatibility, Sentry, and telemetry passed. The final Greptile-only local gate correctly refused before a committed diff existed; it will be executed against the pushed PR head in the required review loop."
  "2026-07-31T10:17:25.528Z","harness:codex","Algorithm governance checkpoint 2026-07-31: the existing catalog and pm-g8t7b9 remain the canonical home for proposed scoring/memory mechanisms; no parallel algorithm item was created. Completion is explicitly gated on the independent pm-atfm evaluator and the portfolio disposition registry. Candidate generation, multi-signal scoring, diversity-aware marginal utility per token, omission costs, uncertainty/trust disclosure, deterministic packing, feedback, counterfactual replay, and million-item index execution must remain separable SDK contracts so extensions can replace one layer without forking context assembly."
  "2026-08-01T20:21:47.721Z","harness:codex","Context algorithm portfolio 2026-08-01: candidate generation combines lifecycle readiness, ownership and claim affinity, BM25 or embedding retrieval, incremental metadata indexes, typed graph neighborhoods, critical-path slack, recent semantic events, and returning-agent deltas. Ranking uses calibrated signals for urgency, dependency impact, goal and milestone reachability, semantic match, historical continuity, confidence, risk, novelty, and diversity; updated_at alone is never a freshness oracle. Selection is a budgeted submodular or knapsack problem with hard inclusions for owned work, blockers, decisions, and safety evidence, followed by maximum-marginal-relevance diversity and deterministic tie-breaking. Output degrades by field groups and row count with omission receipts and score explanations bounded by the answer, not workspace size. Tiny workspaces use an exact scan; medium workspaces use local derived indexes; million-item and fleet modes use incremental content-addressed shards, cursor deltas, shared attestations, and cache invalidation by immutable event identity. Quality gates measure required-item recall, NDCG, continuity coverage, tokens per useful fact, latency, memory, stability, fairness across item types, and adversarial stale or poisoned metadata."
notes[66]:
  - created_at: "2026-07-07T15:12:12.161Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm brainstorm 2026-07-07 (planning pass) — concrete candidate algorithms for the scorer, extending the body's signal list with the combination machinery:\n(1) Scale-free recency via ACT-R base-level activation: activation(i) = ln(sum over accesses t_j of t_j^-d), d~0.5, computed from the item's history stream timestamps. Log-time decay is the one law that behaves identically for a minutes-old scratch workspace and a decades-old archive (the spectrum invariant pm-z436/pm-g2zz demand) and it rewards frequency AND recency in one term, replacing ad-hoc half-life constants.\n(2) Spreading activation for associative recall: seed activation at the focus item, active claims, and current plan step; propagate over typed edges (blocked_by/parent/related plus semantic k-NN edges from the hybrid index) with per-edge-type damping; k iterations of sparse matrix-vector products over the derived read index keeps it O(neighborhood), never O(workspace).\n(3) Learned signal weights with explainability preserved: keep the final score a LINEAR combination of the per-signal contributions (so --explain-ranking stays truthful), but fit the weights per workspace offline against the pm-atfm eval harness as the objective (pairwise learning-to-rank on 'agent actually used this item next' labels derivable from history streams). Weights persist in settings; default weights ship for cold start.\n(4) Token-budget packing as submodular selection: greedy by marginal-value-per-token (MMR-style redundancy penalty so two near-duplicate items do not both spend budget), with per-item projection degradation levels (full body -> summary -> id+title) as fractional knapsack steps. Greedy is provably near-optimal for submodular objectives and is deterministic.\nAll four stay SDK-side behind scoreContextCandidates; none require new stored state beyond what history streams and the read index already hold."
  - created_at: "2026-07-07T15:26:05.199Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm brainstorm 2026-07-07 (planning pass, continuation of (1)-(4)):\n(5) Reciprocal rank fusion as the cold-start combiner: before a workspace has enough history for (3)'s learned linear weights, combining per-signal RANKS instead of scores — score(i) = sum over signals s of 1/(k + rank_s(i)), k~60 — needs no calibration between signals with incompatible scales (activation vs cosine similarity vs priority ordinals), is deterministic, and is robust to any one signal degenerating (a broken/empty signal ranks everything equally and cancels out). Graduation path: ship RRF as the zero-config default, fit (3)'s linear weights per workspace once pm-atfm labels exist, keep RRF as the documented fallback. Explainability holds: per-signal rank contributions are exactly reportable in --explain-ranking.\n(6) Write-time incremental signal maintenance: at millions of items (pm-g2zz) even O(candidates) re-scoring at read time is dominated by recomputing per-item activation/affinity from raw history streams. Because every mutation already lands one immutable history entry, fold signal state incrementally at write time: each history append updates that item's running activation term, author-affinity counters, and semantic-neighborhood invalidation in the derived read index (pm-801d) — an event-sourced materialized view of the scorer inputs. Read-time work drops to combine-and-pack (4); rebuild-from-history stays the recovery path, preserving the derived-never-authoritative invariant. This is also the natural home for the per-author watermarks that pm-vvib candidates #4 novelty and #5 theory-of-mind derive — one fold pass maintains all of them."
  - created_at: "2026-07-07T15:37:10.972Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm brainstorm 2026-07-07 (planning pass, continuation of (1)-(6)):\n(7) Two-stage retrieval funnel with per-source candidate quotas — (1)-(6) specify how to SCORE and PACK, but at millions of items (pm-g2zz) the scorer must never see the whole workspace; the missing explicit piece is the recall stage of the classic recall-then-rank architecture. Candidate generation unions bounded top-k slates from independent cheap sources, each with its own quota: (a) ANN top-k from the hybrid semantic index, (b) n-hop graph expansion from focus/claims/plan steps (the seed set of (2)), (c) top-k by folded activation from the write-time view of (6) (a simple sorted structure makes this O(k)), (d) the agent's own WIP/claimed set and watermark-delta (novelty candidates), (e) exact lexical/field-predicate hits. Per-source quotas guarantee slate DIVERSITY structurally (no single degenerate signal floods the pool) and make cost independent of workspace size: scoring is O(sum of quotas), never O(items). Quotas scale with the token budget, which is how the same funnel serves a 3-item scratch workspace (every source returns everything, union is the whole workspace) and a decades-deep archive unchanged — the spectrum invariant. Explainability extends naturally: --explain-ranking reports which source(s) recalled each candidate before the per-signal score breakdown. SDK-side behind scoreContextCandidates like (1)-(6); composes directly with (5) RRF, which can fuse the per-source ranks as its cold-start combiner."
  - created_at: "2026-07-07T15:57:35.738Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm brainstorm 2026-07-07 (planning pass, continuation of (1)-(7)):\n(8) Sketch-backed scorer state for million-item scale — (6)'s write-time fold keeps per-item activation and per-author affinity counters, but at the pm-g2zz extreme (millions of items x thousands of concurrent authors) exact per-(author, item/tag) counter matrices become the new scale bottleneck: state grows O(authors x touched-items) and dominates the derived read index. Replace exact counters with probabilistic sketches, each with provable error bounds: (a) Count-Min sketch for author-affinity and tag/field co-occurrence counts (sub-linear memory, point-query overestimate bounded by epsilon*N with tunable width/depth); (b) HyperLogLog for distinct-cardinality signals (how many distinct authors/sessions touched this subtree — a trust/centrality input) at ~1.5KB per counter regardless of cardinality; (c) exponential-decay variants (time-decayed CM sketch) so ACT-R-style recency folding from (1) composes directly — decay applied at fold time, no rescan. Spectrum-neutral by construction: below a size threshold the fold stores EXACT counters and the sketch path is dormant; the switchover is an internal representation change invisible to --explain-ranking consumers (signal semantics identical, only precision epsilon differs, and the envelope can disclose 'approximate' when sketched). Derived-never-authoritative holds: sketches are read-index state, rebuildable from immutable history streams at any time; error compounds only within a rebuild epoch. Composes with (7)'s funnel — per-source top-k slates read sketch summaries, never raw streams — and with (5) RRF, which consumes ranks and is inherently robust to bounded count error. SDK-side behind scoreContextCandidates like (1)-(7)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-07T16:35:16.531Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm brainstorm 2026-07-07 (planning pass, continuation of (1)-(8)):\n(9) Granularity-adaptive packing — coarse-to-fine representation selection under one token budget. (4) treats each candidate as an atomic (gain, cost) pair, but every item admits MULTIPLE representations with monotone cost/value: id-line (~10 tokens) < compact row (~25) < row+AC/deps (~80) < full body (~hundreds), and a PARENT chunk rollup can stand in for an entire sibling subtree (pm-vvib candidate (10) chunking). Formulate packing as a multiple-choice knapsack: for each candidate (or subtree) pick exactly ONE representation level or drop it. Per-level value curves are concave (diminishing returns per token), so the greedy density heuristic of (4) extends cleanly: start with every survivor at its cheapest level, compute marginal value-per-token for each LEVEL UPGRADE, and greedily buy upgrades until the budget is spent. Properties: (a) no cliff — an item that flat packing would cut entirely degrades to its id-line instead, so the agent at least knows it exists (the human gist-memory analog: recall THAT before recall WHAT); (b) chunk substitution is just another level priced at the subtree's rollup size, giving structure-aware compression for free; (c) explainability (3) is preserved — each served row can carry its level plus why it was not upgraded (budget exhausted vs low marginal density). Composes with the funnel (7): recall produces the slate, (1)-(5) value it, granularity packing spends the budget; rollup sizes/digests are maintained by the write-time fold (6) so packing stays read-cheap. Spectrum behavior: small workspaces have budget exceeding full-granularity cost, output identical to today; million-item workspaces emit a few full-detail items + chunk digests + an id-line gist tail, in O(slate x levels, log-factor for the upgrade heap) time."
  - created_at: "2026-07-07T20:06:11.214Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm (10) structural fingerprints for analogical recall (Weisfeiler-Leman relational hashing). Serves pm-vvib candidate subsystem (11): retrieve past episodes whose relational SHAPE matches the current situation, independent of vocabulary. Index side: for every item, extract its typed local neighborhood (parent/child edges, dep kinds, status-transition sequence from history, close_reason class, link types) and run k rounds of WL label refinement — each round rehashes an item label with the multiset of its neighbors' labels, so after k rounds the label encodes the k-hop relational shape. The resulting multiset of subtree hashes is the item's structural fingerprint. Similarity = weighted Jaccard over fingerprints; MinHash + LSH banding gives sublinear candidate lookup, so analogical recall is one more bounded-quota source in the two-stage funnel (7), fused via RRF (5) with semantic and associative channels. Costs compose with the existing plan: fingerprints fold incrementally at write time (6) — a mutation re-refines only the k-hop neighborhood it touched; sketch-backed state (8) bounds index size at million-item scale; packing (4)/(9) treats a retrieved precedent as one candidate whose payload is the episode digest (resolution + top learning), never the raw subgraph. Explainability preserved (3): the matched WL subtree hashes decode to which relations aligned (\"both had a wontfix blocker feeding a release-gated dependent\"), rendered as one line in --explain-ranking. Spectrum behavior: small workspaces have few distinct shapes so candidates collapse into recency/semantic ranking (signal silent, zero cost); decades-scale workspaces get case-based precedent recall no content signal can produce. Distinct from (2) spreading activation (connectivity along EXPLICIT shared edges; this matches shape between DISCONNECTED regions) and from embedding similarity (content, not structure)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-07T20:26:24.821Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm (11) open-loop tension scoring with decay floors (Zeigarnik persistence). Serves pm-vvib candidate subsystem (12): incomplete work must persist in assembled context until completed or deliberately parked, never fade under recency decay. Mechanism: maintain a per-agent open-loop registry — folded incrementally at write time (6) like all derived state — holding every loop the agent owns: claimed items, Decisions without recorded resolution, notes ending in unanswered questions, reviews awaiting reply. Each loop carries a tension score composed of deadline proximity, blocker fan-in (how many items the loop blocks — consumes the dep graph), decision-pending markers, and a claim-age shape that rises then plateaus, so ancient abandoned WIP hands off to stale-WIP hygiene (pm-w8q4) instead of dominating forever. Tension acts as a FLOOR under the salience scorer's decay term (1): an open loop's effective salience never drops below its tension, so it cannot be evicted by token-budget packing (4)/(9) while live. Release events — close, claim release, explicit parking (blocked-with-reason) — zero the floor and return the item to ordinary consolidation-managed scoring; each release is itself an entry in the immutable history stream, so the registry rebuilds exactly from replay. Bounded by design: the registry is capped per agent at working-memory scale (~7 loops, composing with chunking (9): overflow loops collapse under their parent chunk), so cost is O(active agents), independent of workspace size; sketch-backed state (8) is unnecessary here because the set is intrinsically small. Explainability (3): floored items render one line in --explain-ranking — held: open loop, claimed 3d ago, blocks 2, decision pending. Spectrum behavior: small workspaces never hit packing limits so the floor is a silent no-op; million-item thousand-agent workspaces guarantee every agent resumes with its own unfinished business first at zero scan cost. Distinct from (2) spreading activation (cue-driven associative boost; this is cue-INDEPENDENT persistence) and from (7) funnel quotas (source selection before fusion; this is a rank floor after fusion)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-08T04:50:57.291Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm (12) co-change coupling mining + outcome-conditioned precedent projection (episodic future thinking; serves pm-vvib candidate 13). Two composable parts. Part A — evolutionary coupling from episodic streams: mine 'items/files that change together' association statistics directly from the immutable history (mining-software-repositories co-change coupling): for item pairs (i,j), maintain support = |sessions touching both| and confidence(i→j) = support / |sessions touching i|, where a 'session' is a same-author time-windowed burst of mutations (or an explicit focus session). Computed as a WRITE-TIME FOLD (algorithm 6) into a count-min-sketch-backed pair table (algorithm 8) so cost is O(burst width) per mutation and storage stays sublinear at millions of items; decay counts with ACT-R log-time activation (algorithm 1) so decades-old coupling fades without a cliff. This yields the tracker's latent 'hidden dependency' graph — edges nobody declared but history proves, exactly the edges that predict surprise breakage. Part B — outcome-conditioned precedent projection: given a target item (about to be claimed / planned), retrieve top-k precedents by WL structural fingerprint (algorithm 10) UNIONED with top-k by co-change neighborhood overlap from Part A, then score each precedent by outcome relevance: divergence signals from its close record (expected_result vs actual_result mismatch, reopen events, close_reason class, time-to-close vs estimate from pm-3hps calibration data). Emit (a) predicted blast radius = co-change neighbors above a confidence floor that are NOT in the declared dep/parent/file neighborhood (candidate advisory edges, feeding the pm-4ri6 dedupe/advisory surface), and (b) top failure precedents with one-line 'how it actually ended' summaries for the pm context / pm plan pre-mortem block. Both parts are recall-stage sources for the two-stage funnel (algorithm 7, new quota source) and their contributions stay linear/explainable in the scorer (algorithm 3). Spectrum-invariant: sketch + log decay for the millions-of-items end (pm-g2zz), degenerates gracefully to exact small tables under the pm-z436 small-end profile."
  - created_at: "2026-07-08T04:59:00.593Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Algorithm (13) salience-gated broadcast arbitration with habituation (global-workspace competition; serves pm-vvib candidate 14). Purpose: in a workspace with thousands of concurrent agents, decide WHICH few events earn workspace-wide attention and for HOW LONG, without a daemon and without flooding every context envelope. Mechanism, three stages. Stage 1 — local salience scoring at write time: every mutation event already folds derived state (algorithm 6); the same fold computes a broadcast score as an explainable linear combination (algorithm 3) of: surprise (inverse of the event's predicted probability given co-change statistics, algorithm 12 Part A — an edit nobody's coupling history predicted scores high), blast radius (count of items/agents whose open loops (11) or claimed WIP sit inside the event's co-change + dep neighborhood), outcome severity (negativity-weighted: failure/wontfix/regression close-reasons outrank routine closes, pm-vvib candidate 7), and deadline pressure (proximity of release-gated dependents). Stage 2 — bounded workspace arbitration: a fixed-capacity broadcast set (~4 slots, human working-memory scale) maintained as a fold over the event stream (pm-e200 substrate, ADR pm-i7i4 no-daemon: arbitration re-evaluates lazily whenever any agent next assembles context); an event enters only by exceeding the weakest incumbent, and incumbents decay by ACT-R log-time activation (1) so the channel self-clears. Stage 3 — habituation / inhibition-of-return: repeated structurally similar events (same WL fingerprint class, algorithm 10) attenuate geometrically so one flaky gate cannot monopolize a slot, and an agent's acknowledgment (an ordinary immutable history event) suppresses re-serving THAT event to THAT agent while leaving it live for others — per-agent delivery state is derivable from each agent's own episodic stream, zero new authoritative state. Costs: O(1) score per mutation amortized via the existing fold; arbitration is O(slots) per context assembly; sketch-backed fingerprint classes (8) bound habituation memory at million-item scale. Explainability (3): each broadcast slot renders one line with its winning terms (\"broadcast: wontfix close blocks 3 release-gated items, 2 agents in radius\"). Spectrum behavior: single-agent or low-traffic workspaces never fill slot 1 above threshold — the block is absent and costs nothing; thousand-agent workspaces get coherent shared attention (no duplicated discovery, no missed cross-lane hazards) for ~4 lines of tokens per envelope. Distinct from (2) spreading activation (cue-driven, per-focus recall boost; this is cue-independent workspace-wide push), from (11) open-loop floors (persistence of MY unfinished work; this is arbitration among EVERYONE's events), and from the retrieval funnel (7) quotas (which select candidate items per source; this selects events for a dedicated always-on attention block)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-08T22:51:13.305Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-08 (third follow-up): Algorithm (14) retrieval-conditioned contradiction gating for memory reconsolidation (serves pm-vvib candidate 15). Purpose: decide WHEN a retrieved precedent becomes eligible for an append-only correction delta, so the corpus self-corrects under use without thrashing on every read. Mechanism, three stages. Stage 1 — retrieval events as cheap immutable facts: whenever context assembly (algorithm 7 funnel) serves a precedent item into an agent's envelope, fold a (retriever, precedent_id, ts) retrieval event into sketch state (algorithm 8) — O(1), no daemon, replayable from the streams. Stage 2 — contradiction scoring at the NEXT write: when the retrieving agent later records an outcome on its own item (close with resolution/expected/actual, a learning, a decision), compute an explainable linear contradiction score (algorithm 3 weights) between that outcome and each recently-retrieved precedent: (a) outcome divergence — precedent's recorded resolution vs the new expected-vs-actual pair (categorical mismatch = strong signal); (b) estimate divergence — |log(actual_minutes/estimated_minutes)| of the new work when the precedent supplied the estimate prior; (c) semantic opposition — embedding distance flip between precedent decision text and the new learning text (reuses the existing vector index, no new store); (d) structural relevance gate — WL fingerprint similarity (algorithm 10) between the two work contexts, so only contradictions in STRUCTURALLY SIMILAR situations count (a divergent outcome in an unrelated context is noise, not correction pressure). Eligibility opens only when score crosses a threshold AND the habituation guard passes: per (retriever, precedent) pair at most one open window per K retrievals, mirroring the biological constraint that reconsolidation needs a labile window, not constant rewriting. Stage 3 — correction pressure as a fold-time retrieval signal: accepted deltas append as ordinary immutable events pairing (precedent_id, retrieving_context_id, correction); write-time maintenance (algorithm 6) folds a decaying correction-pressure counter per precedent that (i) demotes the precedent's retrieval rank proportionally, floored so originals never disappear (immutability + auditability), and (ii) attaches the N most recent corrections whenever the precedent IS served (candidate 15 acceptance shape). Confirmations (retrievals followed by consistent outcomes) fold the opposite way and harden rank — spaced retrieval-practice strengthening (pm-uwfs territory) composes here rather than duplicating. Spectrum behavior: small workspaces rarely cross threshold, cost is one sketch fold per retrieval; million-item workspaces get a queryable relitigation surface — precedents with sustained correction pressure are exactly the decisions the organisation keeps re-arguing, surfaced via ordinary list/search over folded metadata rather than a batch job."
  - created_at: "2026-07-08T22:59:05.110Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-09 (fourth follow-up): Algorithm (15) age-and-value-conditioned verbatim decay with gist-preserving compaction (serves pm-vvib candidate 16 fuzzy-trace dual representation). Purpose: decide WHICH items and WHEN to compress from verbatim to gist so the CORPUS itself stays token-cheap to hold, without ever destroying verbatim or breaking replay — the storage-side dual of read-time packing (9), which selects representation per QUERY. Distinct from (9): (9) chooses a level to SERVE and leaves bytes untouched; (15) chooses bytes to MOVE behind a replayable checkpoint so the default-served layer is smaller for everyone, permanently. Mechanism, three stages. Stage 1 — eligibility scoring (explainable linear form, algorithm 3 weights), computed only for CLOSED, quiescent items so live/open work is never compacted: (a) age since closed_at (needs pm-m4iu; until then falls back to last-history-event ts, never updated_at — the drag-trap field is banned here); (b) quiescence — no reads/retrievals (algorithm 8 sketch retrieval counters from candidate-15 stage 1) and no reconsolidation corrections (candidate 15) in the last K epochs, so precedents under active relitigation stay full-fidelity; (c) verbatim mass — bytes recoverable by compaction (long bodies/comment threads/raw history) so effort targets high-yield items; (d) NEGATIVE weight for structural centrality — items that are frequent analogical precedents (algorithm 10 WL fingerprint hub-ness) or high co-change hubs (algorithm 12) keep verbatim longer because their detail is repeatedly reused. Stage 2 — gist synthesis with provenance: for an eligible item, fold a durable gist digest from the fields fuzzy-trace theory says survive — title, close_reason, resolution, expected/actual, top structural edges, and a bounded extractive summary of the body — and stamp it with a provenance pointer to the exact history-compact checkpoint the verbatim was moved behind. The gist is itself an immutable derived event (write-time fold, algorithm 6); verbatim bytes are relocated by history-compact, NOT deleted, so get --at <version> reconstructs full verbatim on demand and the hash chain still verifies (history-compact's existing replay-integrity guarantee is the substrate; this algorithm only supplies the scheduling policy and the gist payload). Stage 3 — divergence guard (the false-memory detector candidate 16 requires): whenever verbatim is later reconstructed (an agent expands an item), cheaply re-derive the gist from the reconstructed verbatim and compare to the stored gist digest; a mismatch beyond tolerance means the gist drifted from its source (bad summary, or a reconsolidation correction that should have updated the gist but did not) and is surfaced as a validate/health signal keyed to the provenance pointer — never silently trusted. Un-provenanced gist (a digest with no checkpoint pointer) is likewise a hard validate finding. Composes: retrieval funnel (7) serves gist by default; packing (9) prices verbatim expansion as the top representation level; reconsolidation corrections (algorithm 14) attach at the gist layer so they survive compaction; chunking (algorithm 9 rollups) can pack a parent's gist over children's gists for structure-aware compression of compressed items. Spectrum behavior: small/young workspaces have no closed-and-quiescent items so eligibility never fires — one cheap fold per close, zero compaction, full verbatim always; million-item multi-decade workspaces converge to a working set of full-verbatim live/central items plus a long tail of gist-only closed items at a fraction of the bytes, each expandable and hash-verifiable on demand. Cost: O(closed-and-newly-quiescent items per epoch) for eligibility, all folded at write time / gc time (no daemon, ADR pm-i7i4); divergence checks are pay-on-expand, O(1) per reconstruction."
  - created_at: "2026-07-08T23:04:09.486Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-09 (fifth follow-up): Algorithm (16) successor-representation predictive map over agent trajectories (serves pm-vvib candidate 17 cognitive map). Purpose: give agents ORIENTATION — region, landmarks, goal direction, latent shortcuts — as folded derived state, the computational account of hippocampal cognitive maps (Dayan's successor representation; Stachenfeld et al.: place cells encode expected discounted future state occupancy, grid cells are the SR's eigenvectors). Mechanism, three stages. Stage 1 — trajectory extraction: each agent's episodic stream already records the ordered sequence of items it touches (mutations, plus retrieval events from algorithm 14 stage 1); fold same-author time-windowed transitions i→j at write time (algorithm 6), O(1) per mutation, replayable from the immutable streams. Stage 2 — SR maintenance: maintain M(i,j) ≈ expected discounted future occupancy of j given presence at i via incremental TD(λ)-style updates per folded transition; at scale, never hold the full matrix — keep a top-d spectral embedding (the \"grid-cell\" basis) with sketch-backed state (algorithm 8), so storage is O(items × d) and updates stay local; ACT-R log-time decay (algorithm 1) ages transitions so decade-old traffic patterns fade without cliffs. Stage 3 — map services, all read-time and bounded: (a) ORIENTATION — regions emerge as clusters in SR-embedding space (traversal-defined, complementing declared-hierarchy chunking in algorithm 9); landmarks = highest stationary-occupancy hubs (milestone/epics/ADRs surface naturally); the pm context orientation block renders focus item's region, nearest landmarks with hop counts, and expected-steps-to-goal. (b) GOAL-CONDITIONED DISTANCE — expected traversal steps between focus and the agent's claimed milestone read directly off the SR, a principled next-step input to pm next's ranking (which item moves me toward my goal, not just which is globally ready). (c) SHORTCUT INFERENCE — high SR similarity between items/regions with NO declared edge and NO co-change support = a latent route worth surfacing as an advisory edge (pm-4ri6 surface); complements algorithm 12 co-change (pairwise same-burst coupling) because SR composes MULTI-STEP discounted paths — it predicts where work LEADS, not just what it touches together. (d) ZOOM — coarse SR eigenvectors give large-scale regions, finer ones give local structure, exactly the multi-scale levels candidate 17 serves; packing (algorithms 4/9) prices each zoom level like any representation level. Explainability (3): every orientation line decodes to its occupancy statistics (\"region: release-tooling — you touched 7 of its items this week; landmark pm-doxj, 2 hops; goal ~4 steps via pm-wrbe\"). Distinct from (2) spreading activation (one-shot cue diffusion at read time over explicit edges; SR is a LEARNED long-horizon expectation folded at write time), from (12) co-change mining (pairwise burst statistics; SR is sequential, discounted, and composes routes), and from (10) WL fingerprints (static structural shape; SR is dynamic traversal statistics — two identically-shaped subgraphs can occupy opposite ends of the traffic map). Spectrum behavior: small/young workspaces — transitions are too sparse for regions to form, SR stays near-diagonal, orientation block suppressed below threshold, cost one fold per mutation; million-item thousand-agent workspaces — the map is the only sublinear way for a cold agent to orient and route without scans, and per-agent trajectories fold independently so concurrency needs no coordination beyond the existing append-only streams."
  - created_at: "2026-07-09T10:35:29.359Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-09 (sixth follow-up): Algorithm (17) learning-progress-guided gap scheduling — an epistemic-foraging queue (serves pm-vvib candidate 18 curiosity drive). Purpose: rank what the workspace should LEARN next, so exploration (filling knowledge gaps) gets a principled, bounded share of agent attention alongside exploitation (pm next's ready work), using only signals that already exist. Mechanism, three stages. Stage 1 — gap-signal folding at write time (algorithm 6): maintain per-item and per-region (regions from algorithm 16 SR clusters, falling back to parent subtree via algorithm 9 chunking when the SR is too sparse) an uncertainty vector with four families: (a) completeness — count of absent high-value fields (AC, risk, confidence, resolution triple, close_reason), weighted by the item's retrieval frequency (algorithm 14 stage-1 sketch counters) so gaps in items agents actually USE outrank gaps in dead corners; (b) prediction residuals — per-region rolling error of the predictive layers (co-change algorithm 12: predicted-but-untouched and touched-but-unpredicted; SR algorithm 16: realized-trajectory divergence), folded as decaying counters (algorithm 1); (c) reliability blindness — retrieval-without-outcome ratio per precedent (served k times, zero confirmations/corrections from algorithm 14 stage 2), i.e. trust with no evidence; (d) isolation — hybrid-index neighborhood sparsity below a floor (no near neighbors = unreachable by association = invisible to spreading activation (2)). All sketch-backed (algorithm 8), O(1) amortized per mutation, replayable, no daemon (ADR pm-i7i4). Stage 2 — learning-progress estimator (the curiosity core): per region, track the DERIVATIVE of each error family over fold epochs; score curiosity as an explainable linear combination (algorithm 3) of gap magnitude × |error slope|, gated by the inverted-U: suppress mastered regions (error low and flat — nothing to learn) and unlearnable ones (error high and flat for M epochs — more sampling will not help; typically means missing instrumentation or genuinely chaotic territory, flagged once to validate instead of burning agent tokens). This is Oudeyer-style learning progress applied to a work corpus: attention flows to where the map is improvably wrong, and automatically withdraws as a region is either mastered or written off. Stage 3 — bounded exploration serving: the gap queue becomes one additional quota source in the retrieval funnel (algorithm 7) and an opt-in section of pm next — at most ONE gap-fill suggestion per envelope, rendered with its winning terms (\"gap: release-tooling region — 12 retrieved-without-confirmation precedents, prediction error fell 40% last epoch, top item pm-XXXX missing risk+AC\"), each suggestion a concrete enrichment action (add the missing field, confirm/correct the precedent, link the isolated item). Serving folds a curiosity-visit event so the same gap is not re-served while an agent works it (habituation, algorithm 13 stage 3 pattern; inhibition-of-return per agent derivable from its own stream). Explicitly NOT a new item generator by default: suggestions are actions on EXISTING items — item creation stays a human/agent decision, keeping the no-duplicates invariant intact. Distinct from (13) broadcast arbitration (pushes currently-salient EVENTS; this pulls toward stable KNOWLEDGE deficits), from (14) contradiction gating (corrects precedents PROVEN wrong; this targets precedents whose reliability is UNKNOWN), and from validate (static rule findings at a point in time; this is a dynamic, usage-weighted, progress-gated ranking over those findings plus model residuals). Spectrum behavior: small/young workspaces — error series too short, inverted-U gate keeps the quota empty, cost one fold per mutation; million-item decades-old workspaces — the corpus self-heals where it is used most, and the unlearnable-region flag gives maintainers a map of where instrumentation (not effort) is the missing ingredient. Completes the exploit/explore pair: algorithms 1–16 optimize serving what is known; 17 allocates attention to what is not."
  - created_at: "2026-07-09T11:41:16.021Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Synthesis 2026-07-09 (planning pass): dependency ordering of algorithms (1)-(17) onto the four existing implementation slices, replacing further territory scouting (catalog complete at 17).\n\nOWNERSHIP MAP — every algorithm has exactly one owning slice:\n- pm-h3no (scorer contract): (5) RRF cold-start combiner ships as the zero-config default combiner inside the contract; (3) learned linear weights is the contract's documented graduation path (needs pm-uwfs labels + pm-atfm objective); (1) ACT-R activation defines the contract's canonical signal shape.\n- pm-3hps (signal feature store): (1) activation computation, (2) spreading activation over typed edges, (6) write-time incremental fold (event-sourced scorer state; also home of the per-author watermarks candidates #4/#5 need), (8) sketch-backed state at million-item scale, and the mined signals (10) WL fingerprints, (12) co-change coupling, (16) successor-representation map as late additions.\n- pm-55ra (packer): (4) submodular greedy packing, (9) granularity-adaptive coarse-to-fine projection, (7) two-stage retrieval funnel with per-source quotas (the packer's candidate-supply side; also the quota surface (17) plugs into).\n- pm-uwfs (usage feedback): label source for (3) and the learning-progress denominator for (17).\nLifecycle/governance algorithms (11) tension floors, (13) broadcast arbitration, (14) contradiction gating, (15) gist compaction, (17) gap scheduling stay ADR-gated (pm-vvib) — they get owning items only on acceptance, wired to the fold (6) and funnel (7) surfaces.\n\nWAVE ORDER (encoded in the graph today: pm-3hps and pm-55ra now blocked_by pm-h3no):\n- Wave 1 — first implementation slice, read-time only, zero new stored state: contract (pm-h3no) with (1)+(5)+(4): activation from history timestamps, RRF combination, greedy budget packing. Deterministic, explainable, small-workspace neutral — directly testable against the pm-4k6b AC golden tests.\n- Wave 2 — retrieval scale: (7) funnel + (2) spreading activation + (9) granularity packing; needs pm-801d read-index edges and pm-dfg0 pagination.\n- Wave 3 — write-time state: (6) fold, then (8) sketches; unlocks novelty/theory-of-mind watermarks and (11)/(13) counters.\n- Wave 4 — mined and learned: (3) weights, (10), (12), (16); all consume wave-3 fold infrastructure plus pm-atfm/pm-uwfs.\n- Wave 5 — lifecycle governance: (14), (15), (17); gated on pm-vvib acceptance and waves 2-3 surfaces.\n\nRecommendation: wave 1 is ADR-independent and unblocked once pm-rjqr/pm-x6jf SDK promotion lands — it is the natural next engineering start on this epic."
  - created_at: "2026-07-10T10:35:24.600Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Synthesis 2026-07-10 (wave checkpoint): WAVE 1 SHIPPED. PR #487 (merged 2026-07-09, evidence comments 2026-07-10) closed pm-h3no (public scorer contract, deterministic defaults, RRF-style combining as zero-config default, context+next integration, governed extension override), pm-xmp5 (rank-aware eval runner + CI gate — the four-scenario corpus gate now runs in CI), and pm-cu1i (token-budget gate). The contract-first ordering from the 2026-07-09 ownership map worked: pm-3hps and pm-55ra blocked_by edges are now satisfied and both slices are actionable. Revised wave order from here: WAVE 2 = pm-3hps signal feature store — algorithms (1) ACT-R activation, (2) spreading over the dep/parent graph, (6) write-time fold — derived read-path state only, feeding the shipped contract's canonical signal shape; grow pm-lixy corpus scenarios alongside so each new signal lands with graded judgments. WAVE 3 = pm-55ra packer — (4) budget packing, (9) granularity projection, (7) retrieval funnel — consumes store signals, so it stays after wave 2. WAVE 4 = pm-uwfs usage feedback ((3) learned-weight graduation labels + (17) learning-progress scheduling input). WAVE 5 = lifecycle algorithms (11)(13)(14)(15) unchanged: gated on pm-vvib ADR acceptance (package staged in pm-vvib note 2026-07-09, still awaiting maintainer review as of 2026-07-10). pm-atfm (P1) remains the umbrella eval harness for waves 2-4; pm-mi2x scale benchmark becomes load-bearing at wave 3 (packer is the first slice with superlinear cost risk)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-10T10:46:55.944Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Synthesis 2026-07-10 (wave-frontier correction): the 2026-07-10 wave checkpoint overstated actionability. Live dependency state: pm-3hps (wave 2 store) is blocked_by pm-801d (OPEN, P2, ~900min persistent derived read index, scale epic pm-9rxu child; edge added by codex-agent 2026-07-06) in addition to the satisfied pm-rjqr/pm-h3no edges. pm-55ra (wave 3 packer) is blocked_by pm-atfm (OPEN eval harness) and pm-dfg0 (OPEN cursor pagination) in addition to satisfied pm-cu1i/pm-h3no. Only the pm-h3no edges were satisfied by wave 1. Consequence: the true wave-2 entry set is pm-801d (or an owner decision to soften that edge to related). Recommendation A (preferred): treat pm-801d as wave 2a and co-design its derived read-path state layout with the pm-3hps signal store (see design-constraints note on pm-3hps 2026-07-10) so one derived-state substrate serves both, then land pm-3hps as wave 2b. Recommendation B: pm-801d's owner downgrades the edge to related, accepting scan-based signal computation at current (~1.7k item) scale and deferring the index; the store contract must then be written so the index can slide underneath later without a signal-shape change. Do not remove the codex-agent edge unilaterally. pm-atfm as blocker of pm-55ra is a soft ordering edge (harness should exist before the packer to measure it) and pm-dfg0 is a real contract dependency (packer emits bounded output via the same cursor/bounded-output contract)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-12T19:06:43.973Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-12 (directed maintainer pass): Algorithm (18) deadline-aware anytime assembly — latency-budgeted top-k fusion with early termination and disclosed completeness. Purpose: the catalog prices context in TOKENS ((4)/(9)) and bounds candidate COUNT ((7) quotas), but nothing yet makes assembly adaptive in TIME — \"optimal context at any possible time\" requires pm context/next to return the best answer computable within an explicit latency budget, at any workspace size, improving monotonically when given more time, never blocking an agent turn on a cold cache or a million-item index. Mechanism, three parts. Part 1 — impact-ordered sources with upper bounds: each funnel source ((7)) exposes its slate as a descending-impact iterator carrying a per-source score UPPER BOUND for its unseen remainder (activation slates are already sorted by the write-time fold (6); ANN indexes stream in distance order; graph expansion is BFS by damped depth (2), bound = damping^depth). Part 2 — threshold-algorithm fusion: consume sources round-robin under Fagin TA/NRA semantics — maintain the best-k fused scores ((5) RRF or (3) learned linear, both rank-monotone) and a threshold = fused score of a hypothetical item ranked next in EVERY source; STOP when best-k >= threshold (exact top-k certified without exhausting any source) OR the deadline fires (return best-so-far with the threshold gap disclosed in the envelope as a completeness certificate). Part 3 — degradation ladder under deadline pressure: per-stage budgets (recall/fuse/pack); on overrun, shed in fixed order — (i) shrink per-source quotas, (ii) skip read-time signal recomputation and serve folded state only ((6)/(8)), (iii) drop enrichment blocks (orientation (16), precedents (10)/(12)) before core ranking, (iv) emergency path = claims + open-loop floors (11) + top folded activation, always O(k) straight off the fold. Every shed step is disclosed (\"degraded: semantic source skipped, 40ms budget\") so agents and the pm-atfm harness can distinguish deadline-induced quality loss from ranking regression; --explain-ranking stays truthful (3). Composes: (7) quotas become ceilings, not targets; packing (4)/(9) is greedy so its anytime property is inherited free (any greedy prefix is a valid packing); the cursor/bounded-output contract (pm-dfg0, shipped PR#525) is the wire shape for \"more time available = resume deeper\". Spectrum behavior: small workspaces certify exact top-k in microseconds — the ladder never engages, zero behavior change; million-item decades-deep archives get a hard latency ceiling per envelope with graceful, DISCLOSED degradation instead of timeouts or unbounded scans. Distinct from (7) (bounds WHAT is considered; this bounds WHEN consideration stops, with a certificate), from (4)/(9) (token budget = output size; this is compute budget = answer latency), and from pm-irp1/pm-gt82 startup latency (process boot cost; this is per-query algorithmic termination). Ownership: deadline parameter + fusion loop land with the wave-3 packer (pm-55ra, unblocked since PR#525); impact-ordered iteration is a DESIGN CONSTRAINT on the pm-801d persistent read index layout (noted there) so the index does not lock this out."
  - created_at: "2026-07-12T19:07:07.668Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-12 (directed maintainer pass, continuation): Algorithm (19) task-set-conditioned scoring profiles — intent-aware weight modulation (extends (3), never replaces it). Purpose: \"optimal context at any possible time\" also has a workflow-moment dimension the catalog left implicit: the same workspace state wants DIFFERENT context depending on what the agent is doing right now. At claim time the optimal envelope is forward-looking (precedents (10)/(12), blast radius, hazards); at close time it is backward-looking (the item's own AC, expected-vs-actual priors, sibling resolutions); at plan time it is structural (dep frontier, landmarks (16)); at review time it is contradiction-heavy (belief-revision surfacing, low-confidence neighbors). Cognitive analog: task sets / cognitive control — goal-driven biasing that reconfigures processing weights without changing the underlying pathways. Mechanism: keep the scorer linear and explainable (3); add a PROFILE dimension keyed by declared intent = triggering verb family x focus-item lifecycle phase x session phase (cold-start vs mid-task vs wrap-up). A profile is a weight vector over the SAME signal set plus an enable-mask over enrichment blocks (broadcast (13), orientation (16), pre-mortem (12B)); the envelope discloses the active profile (\"profile: close/terminal\"). Defaults ship as hand-set per-verb-family profiles (zero-config, like (5) RRF); graduation mirrors (3): pm-atfm labels partitioned by triggering intent — \"agent actually used item next\" labels already carry the verb of the following mutation in the episodic stream, so the training partition is a pure derivation, no new stored state. Fallback is a strict generalization hierarchy, never a fork: profile without enough labels -> global learned weights -> RRF. Guards: profiles modulate WEIGHTS and enrichment masks only, never recall correctness — (7) quotas stay intent-independent floors, and safety-class signals (negativity advisories, open-loop floors (11)) are profile-exempt and mask-immune, so no intent can hide a hazard. Spectrum behavior: small workspaces — profiles converge to identical output because packing never binds; million-item workspaces — one corpus serves plan/execute/close/review envelopes each tuned to the moment. Distinct from pm-vvib candidate 9 encoding specificity (write-time situation STAMPS on items; this is read-time weight selection), from (3) alone (per-workspace weights; this indexes them by intent), and from pm-vvib candidate 13 episodic future thinking (one phase-specific BLOCK; this is the general conditioning layer that decides when such blocks render at all). Ownership: profile table + disclosure land with the wave-3 packer/scorer surface (pm-55ra); learned-profile graduation is wave-4 territory (pm-uwfs)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-12T19:07:33.596Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Synthesis 2026-07-12 (directed maintainer pass): maintainer directive received — \"the pm CLI should use highly advanced algorithms to provide the optimal context at any possible time using all available data+metadata+history, optimized from a few-item minutes-old project to millions of items spanning decades\". Reconciliation against the catalog: (a) \"all available data+metadata+history\" — already covered: metadata signals ((3), pm-3hps signal set), graph (2), history streams (1)(6)(12)(16), retrieval events (14), outcome records (12)(15); (b) small->huge spectrum — already covered by the spectrum-invariant clause every algorithm carries (log-time decay (1), exact-vs-sketch switchover (8), quota scaling (7), small-end no-op degeneracy per pm-z436/pm-g2zz); (c) \"at any possible time\" — TWO real gaps found and closed this pass: latency-anytime semantics = NEW algorithm (18) deadline-aware anytime assembly; workflow-moment conditioning = NEW algorithm (19) task-set profiles. Catalog now complete at 19 — future passes extend THIS ledger, never restart the brainstorm. No new pm-vvib candidate filed: (18)/(19) are engine machinery over existing signals, not new memory subsystems; the vvib acceptance package (staged 2026-07-09) is unchanged and still awaits maintainer review. Delivery order unchanged and remains the bottleneck: wave-2 entry = pm-801d (unblocked since PR#525 — bl8x accepted + mi2x closed; pm next's recommended; now carries the impact-ordered-layout design constraint from (18)) -> pm-3hps store; wave 3 = pm-55ra packer (UNBLOCKED since PR#525 — dfg0+h3no closed; owns the (18) deadline contract + (19) profile table); wave 4 = pm-uwfs (owns (19) learned-profile graduation)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-13T17:49:16.342Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-13 (directed maintainer pass): Algorithm (20) temporal non-stationarity mining — change-point era segmentation plus periodicity-aware anticipatory activation. Purpose: the catalog treats decade-deep history as a smooth continuum — log decay (1), age-and-value compaction (15), pm-5qmm age rollups all assume the SAME process generated old and new events. Decades-long workspaces are non-stationary: rewrites, team turnover, process and terminology shifts create ERAS, while stable cadences (release-cut days, month-end, quarterly planning, annual freezes) create CYCLES; optimal context over decades must know which era a precedent came from and which phase of the cycle now is. Cognitive analogs: temporal landmarks / the reminiscence bump (autobiographical memory organizes recall around era boundaries, not uniform time) and circadian/seasonal modulation of memory availability. Part A — online change-point era segmentation: run Bayesian online change-point detection (constant-time per event with standard run-length pruning) over the write-time fold (6) on a handful of population statistics — mutation rate, author-set turnover (sliding Jaccard), vocabulary/embedding-centroid drift, type/status-distribution shift. Detected boundaries partition the timeline into a tiny era table held as fold state (era id, span, signature = top drifted terms + author cohort + dominant components). Consumers: precedent projection (12) discounts cross-era precedents by a per-boundary validity factor and DISCLOSES the gap (\"precedent predates era: 2031-storage-rewrite — validity discounted\"); contradiction gating (14) takes an era-boundary prior (crossing a boundary lowers the evidence threshold for routing a precedent to verify-before-serve); retrieval alignment: per-era term statistics let the hybrid index expand queries with era-local synonyms mined from boundary-adjacent co-occurrence, so drifted vocabulary still reaches old items. Part B — periodicity-aware anticipatory activation: over folded per-item access streams (ring-buffer binned counts, O(1) update), detect stable cycles via autocorrelation/spectral peaks above a significance floor; items with a confirmed period get a forward-looking activation term anticipated(i,t) = amplitude x kernel(phase distance of t to next predicted peak), added as one more explainable linear signal (3) — release-tooling items warm up BEFORE the cut. Strictly additive: (1) stays the backward-looking base, (20B) is the disclosed forecast term. Spectrum behavior: small/young workspaces — no boundary accumulates enough evidence and no cycle passes the floor, both parts emit nothing, zero behavior change; decades-deep archives — era table stays dozens-of-rows small, periodicity state is O(items with recurring access), everything folds at write time (no daemon, ADR pm-i7i4). Distinct from (1) (smooth stationary decay — this adds the structure decay cannot see), from (15)/pm-5qmm (compress by AGE/value — this segments by REGIME; an era boundary is not an age), from (14) alone (pairwise per-precedent evidence; 20A is a population-level prior over ALL cross-era retrieval), from pm-vvib candidate 9 encoding specificity (per-item write-time situation stamps; this is corpus-level temporal structure), and from (16) (relational geography, not time). Ownership: era/periodicity fold state = pm-3hps (wave 3); era-conditioned discounting + query expansion = wave-4 mined territory alongside (12); envelope disclosure follows the (18)/(19) pattern."
  - created_at: "2026-07-13T17:49:39.249Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-13 (directed maintainer pass, continuation): Algorithm (21) hierarchical empirical-Bayes shrinkage — calibrated uncertainty on every scorer estimate. Purpose: every signal the catalog consumes is an ESTIMATE from finite evidence — author affinity from 3 interactions, co-change confidence (12) from 2 co-closures, estimate-accuracy priors from one project's worth of closes. At the small pole (a few items, minutes old) raw maximum-likelihood estimates are noise; and small-sample estimation is a PERMANENT condition, not a cold-start phase, because at the million-item pole the subpopulations that matter (a new author, a new package dir, a fresh era from (20), a new item type) are always small even when the corpus is huge. The catalog handles WEIGHT cold-start ((5) RRF, (3)->(19) fallback hierarchy) but nothing calibrates the per-signal estimates underneath the weights. Cognitive analog: schema/prototype-based inference — when episodic evidence is thin, recall falls back on category-level expectations and blends smoothly toward instance evidence as it accumulates. Mechanism: give every counted/averaged fold statistic a conjugate hierarchical prior along its natural taxonomy — item <- type <- workspace <- shipped global defaults (Beta-Binomial for rates: co-change confidence, reopen probability, retrieval-confirmation rate; Gamma/log-normal for durations: time-to-close vs estimate; Dirichlet for categorical mixes: close_reason classes). Posterior mean is the closed-form precision-weighted blend estimate = (n*xbar + k*prior)/(n + k) — O(1) at fold time, no fitting loop; the pseudo-count k ships as a default per signal family and can graduate per-workspace through (3)'s existing label machinery. Every estimate carries a credible width, and the width is CONSUMED: packing (4)/(9) gets risk-adjusted marginal value (value = mean - lambda*sigma, lambda selectable per profile (19), so an uncertain high-scorer can enter at reduced projection instead of full verbatim); contradiction gating (14) routes wide-interval precedents to verify sooner; anytime certificates (18) widen their fusion bounds honestly under estimate uncertainty; --explain-ranking discloses the evidence basis (\"affinity 0.62 — n=2, shrunk from raw 0.95 toward type prior 0.40\") so agents see why thin evidence is not trusted at face value. Spectrum behavior: a minutes-old three-item workspace sits entirely at its priors — scorer output equals the shipped default ordering, stable and ceremony-free, serving pm-z436's small-end story with NO special code path; at decades/millions, n dominates and shrinkage vanishes asymptotically, while new subpopulations still get calibrated starts forever. Cost: one evidence count + one prior pointer per folded statistic, compatible with (8) — sketch-estimated counts supply n-hat and shrinkage applies on read. Distinct from (5) (rank fusion sidesteps CROSS-signal scale calibration when combining; this calibrates each estimate WITHIN its own scale before any combining), from (3)/(19) (weights over signals; this is the estimates under the weights), from (8) (approximation error injected by sketching at large N; this is sampling error at small n — the opposite pole), and from (18)'s certificate (compute-budget honesty; this is evidence honesty). Ownership: shrinkage forms + (n, prior) fold state = pm-3hps (wave 3); prior-taxonomy defaults extend the shipped scorer-contract defaults (pm-h3no pattern — extension, not reopen); risk-adjusted packing hook = pm-55ra; per-workspace k graduation = pm-uwfs/pm-atfm (wave 4)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-13T17:50:01.162Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-13 (directed maintainer pass, continuation): Algorithm (22) lineage-aware relevance continuity — identity flow across copy/split/merge/supersede. Purpose: decades of restructuring fragment identity — items get copied (pm copy is a first-class verb and already stamps copied_from=<id> into the history message, deterministic provenance in the episodic stream), split into slices, folded into epics, closed-as-superseded and re-filed. Raw per-item signals treat each fragment as a stranger: a predecessor's decade of activation, outcomes, and precedent value is invisible from its successor, so precisely the oldest, most-restructured knowledge — the decades pole of the directive — scores worst. Optimal context must follow the WORK, not the row id. Cognitive analog: object continuity / identity tracking — episodes bind to the persisting entity across renames and transformations, not to its surface label. Part 1 — lineage edges as first-class typed edges: copied_from (promote the existing history-message stamp to a structured edge at write time), split_from/split_into, merged_into, superseded_by at ITEM level (deliberately the same relation vocabulary as pm-vvib's belief-level supersedes on decisions/learnings — same shape, different corpus; coordination, not duplication). Where explicit edges are missing across legacy decades, mine advisory candidates from close-reason cross-references and title/body similarity at close/create adjacency in the episodic stream — reusing pm-4ri6's hybrid-similarity machinery with a different edge kind, always advisory and confirmable, never auto-linked. Part 2 — relevance mass flow: terminal lineage events transfer a kind-specific fraction of folded activation/affinity to the successor at fold time (6) (merge = capped sum, split = weighted partition, copy = small fraction since the source lives on, supersede = large fraction + tombstone pointer); spreading activation (2) traverses lineage edges with kind-specific damping so residual ancestor mass stays reachable; precedent assembly (10)/(12) collapses a lineage chain into ONE episode — the chain IS the story (filed -> split -> superseded -> done) — instead of k fragmented weak matches; packing (4) treats lineage-mates as near-duplicates so only the best representative spends budget, carrying a lineage breadcrumb for drill-down. Part 3 — identity-collapsed statistics: (21)'s hierarchical priors and (12)'s outcome statistics aggregate over the lineage-collapsed entity (evidence counts sum along the chain), so successors inherit calibrated history instead of restarting at the prior. Spectrum behavior: small/young workspaces have no lineage — zero edges, zero cost, zero behavior change; decades-deep archives get their restructuring history turned from a scoring liability into connective structure; cost is O(lineage events) at fold time and chain collapse is bounded by (2)'s damped hop limit, disclosed. Distinct from pm-g5sx (VCS-branch merging of tracker store files — mechanical JSONL merge safety; this is semantic item identity across time within one store), from vvib's supersedes relation (belief currency for decisions/learnings; this is item-level relevance mass — shared vocabulary by design), from (2) alone (proximity scoring; this adds mass TRANSFER and episode COLLAPSE, not just traversal), from (15) (representation of one item; this is identity across items), and from pm-4ri6 (create-time duplicate prevention; the mining here is post-hoc lineage recovery on the same machinery). Ownership: lineage edge kinds + traversal = design constraint on the pm-801d read index (edge-kind vocabulary must be extensible — recorded there BEFORE implementation); fold-side mass transfer + collapsed statistics = pm-3hps (wave 3); packing dedupe/breadcrumb = pm-55ra; the structured copied_from/superseded_by write-time stamps land with the mutating verbs, sequenced with the wave-3 fold."
  - created_at: "2026-07-13T17:50:19.867Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Synthesis 2026-07-13 (directed maintainer pass): maintainer directive received again — \"highly advanced algorithms for optimal context at any possible time using all data+metadata+history, optimized from a few items spanning seconds/minutes to millions of items spanning several decades\". Per the 2026-07-12 completeness declaration this pass EXTENDED the ledger (never restarted): the two spectrum poles were probed against the 19-catalog and THREE real gaps were found and closed. (a) Decades pole, time axis: the catalog assumed temporal STATIONARITY — NEW algorithm (20) temporal non-stationarity mining (change-point era segmentation + periodicity-aware anticipation). (b) Small pole, evidence axis (also every large-corpus subpopulation): estimates lacked evidence honesty — NEW algorithm (21) hierarchical empirical-Bayes shrinkage with calibrated uncertainty. (c) Decades pole, identity axis: restructuring fragments relevance — NEW algorithm (22) lineage-aware relevance continuity. Considered and REJECTED this pass (do not re-file): (i) a scale-regime query planner / pipeline-morphing layer — already owned by the per-algorithm small-end degeneracy clauses plus (18)'s degradation ladder, which IS the planner under pressure; (ii) an online contextual-bandit weight learner — graduation detail of (3)/(19) owned by pm-uwfs; its one hard prerequisite (envelopes must LOG disclosed profile + rank + inclusion so off-policy evaluation stays possible) is recorded on pm-uwfs; (iii) multi-agent theory-of-mind conditioning — vvib candidates 4/5 territory, dispositioned; (iv) a standalone item-supersedence algorithm — folded INTO (22), sharing vvib's belief-level relation vocabulary. Catalog now complete at 22 — future passes extend THIS ledger. No new pm-vvib candidate: all three are engine machinery over existing signals, not new memory subsystems; the staged vvib acceptance package remains untouched. Wave mapping unchanged in shape: (20)/(21)/(22) fold state = wave 3 (pm-3hps); consumers ((12)/(14) discounting, query expansion, learned k) = wave 4 (pm-uwfs/pm-atfm); packing hooks (risk-adjusted value, lineage dedupe) = pm-55ra; TWO new design constraints recorded on pm-801d (extensible lineage edge-kind vocabulary; optional era tag must not be locked out by the index layout). Delivery order unchanged: pm-801d remains the wave-2 entry and pm next's recommended."
  - created_at: "2026-07-14T16:48:15.645Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Shipped-frontier ledger 2026-07-14 (pass #81), catalog stays COMPLETE at 22 — this note maps catalog to shipped code, no new algorithms. PR#546 shipped pm-55ra token-budget packer: intent profiles (balanced/context/next), diversity + uncertainty penalties, projection degradation ladder (identity/summary/full), required-anchor immunity, completeness accounting — the (18) deadline-aware anytime-assembly contract surface plus the (19) task-set-conditioned profile entry point are now live in pm context / pm next packing summaries. PR#546 also shipped pm-uwfs served-then-touched usage ledger with decayed usage_affinity in the scorer (graduation substrate for learned weighting; opt-out PM_CONTEXT_USAGE_DISABLED=1). PR#559 exported canonical item-to-ContextRelevanceCandidate derivation (pm-qyc6) with deterministic clock/author/usage-affinity/semantic inputs — packages score identically to the CLI. PR#554/#556 shipped the typed relationship graph, event history, and analytics substrate (pm-ju83 lineage under epic pm-96h7) that graph-signal and (22) lineage-continuity scoring will read from; pm-7ob5 analytics + pm-3hps feature store remain the open consumers."
  - created_at: "2026-07-21T15:56:03.505Z"
    author: codex-tracker-governance
    text: "Context objective refresh 2026-07-21: maximize judged relevance, actionability, diversity, continuity, and unresolved-loop coverage minus contradiction risk, uncertainty, latency, and token cost. Hard constraints: deterministic bounded work; provenance and score explanations; inclusion floors for owned open loops and critical risk; graceful tiny-project behavior; and no mutation of source history. Candidate generation should fuse index, lexical or semantic retrieval, recency, claims, graph activation, deadlines, risk, outcomes, and historical analogies before submodular token packing."
  - created_at: "2026-07-24T23:56:51.483Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Catalog entry (23) — consumer-conditioned context shaping. Newly feasible, not a restatement of (19) task-set-conditioned scoring profiles.\n\nMechanism. Every context assembly today optimises for a single implicit consumer with an unknown budget. Once the acting harness is resolved from the runtime (pm-z9x1r2), the assembler knows three facts it has never had: which harness is asking, what its usable context window is, and whether the caller is a terminal human, an MCP tool call, or a piped script. Those facts condition the packer's budget, the projection depth, and the degradation order — not the relevance ranking, which stays consumer-independent.\n\nWhy it is distinct from (19). Entry (19) conditions the scoring weights on the task intent (triage, implement, review). This entry conditions the output shape on the consumer capacity. The same intent asked by a small-window harness and a large-window harness should rank identically and pack differently. Conflating the two would make relevance depend on who is asking, which is the failure mode this separation exists to prevent.\n\nInteraction with the degradation ladder from (18). The anytime ladder decides what to drop under deadline pressure; this entry sets where the budget line sits before the ladder runs. Composition order is: intent-conditioned weights, then relevance ranking, then consumer-conditioned budget, then anytime degradation, then completeness certificate.\n\nSmall-end behaviour. At three-item scale the conditioning collapses to a no-op, because the whole workspace fits under any budget. That is the correct degeneracy and requires no special path, consistent with (21) hierarchical shrinkage serving the small end without a separate mode.\n\nRisk to record. Consumer conditioning is a privacy-adjacent signal: the harness identity must stay local and must never be inferred from telemetry, and the hashed author context that telemetry already carries is the only form permitted to leave the machine.\n\nOwner: this catalog. Delivery is gated on harness resolution (pm-z9x1r2) and the contract-declared output budgets (pm-5t33or) landing first. Dispositioned into the portfolio registry (pm-g8t7b9) rather than filed as a standalone item, per the catalog's standing rule that engine machinery extends the ledger."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T06:21:11.864Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entries (24)-(27), filed 2026-07-25. The catalog was declared complete at (22) and extended by (23); these four close gaps that (1)-(23) structurally cannot reach, because every existing entry scores a single call for a single agent under a known intent. Extend this ledger; never restart it.\n\n(24) Intent-ambiguity hedging via risk-adjusted portfolio selection. Entry (19) conditions the weight vector on a known task intent; entry (4) then packs greedily by marginal value per token. Both assume the intent is identified. In practice the opening call of a session is exactly where intent is least certain, and committing the whole budget to the single most probable intent is the highest-variance play available. Treat selection as portfolio construction over a posterior across intents: each candidate has an expected value under each intent hypothesis, and the objective becomes expected value minus a variance penalty scaled by posterior entropy. Concretely, maintain the intent posterior from the invoking command, recent history, and claim state; compute per-candidate value vectors under each hypothesis; select the subset maximising expected marginal value per token less lambda times the cross-hypothesis variance of that value, with lambda annealing to zero as the posterior sharpens. High-entropy openings therefore buy coverage across plausible intents; sharp posteriors reduce exactly to (19)+(4) with no separate code path. The envelope discloses the posterior and lambda so the hedging is explainable and testable, and pm-atfm gains a natural judgment class: ambiguous-opening scenarios where a hedged pack must beat a committed one.\n\n(25) Minimum-description-length selection with an information-per-token stopping rule. Entries (4) and (9) price redundancy heuristically through marginal value and granularity choice, but neither has a principled place to stop other than the budget ceiling, which means a small workspace still spends its whole allowance. Price context in bits instead: choose the subset that most reduces the description length of the workspace state the agent must reconstruct, and stop when the marginal bits saved per token fall below a declared threshold rather than when the budget is exhausted. This gives three properties nothing else in the catalog provides: a formal account of redundancy (an item that is derivable from already-selected items costs bits and buys none), a natural under-spend on small and unambiguous workspaces which is precisely what pm-z436 needs at three-item scale, and a directly reportable efficiency number - bits per token - that pm-8pnj and the token-cost corpus gate can regress against. The estimator can be cheap: token-level compressibility of the candidate against the already-packed prefix approximates the ideal well enough to rank, and the exact objective stays declared for evaluation.\n\n(26) Fleet-shared cooperative assembly with per-agent personalisation deltas. Every entry so far assembles context for one agent in isolation. At the thousands-of-agents target the packs are massively correlated: agents working the same epic recompute nearly the same candidate set, the same graph traversals, and the same summaries, and each pays full assembly cost. Split assembly into a shared workspace-level layer, versioned and cached against the same substrate the derived read index provides, and a per-agent delta carrying only claim state, authored history, and usage affinity. An agent then pays for its delta plus a reference to a shared layer it may already hold. Two consequences beyond cost: the shared layer is a natural place to record which items the fleet is collectively attending to, which is an input the allocation primitive needs; and a discovery made by one agent - a newly ranked item, a freshly recorded learning - propagates to the fleet through shared-layer invalidation rather than through each agent independently rediscovering it. Correctness requires that the shared layer never leaks another agent's uncommitted intent, so the delta boundary is a governance boundary and not only a caching one.\n\n(27) Session-level budget allocation across turns. Budgets are currently declared per command, which optimises each call in isolation and systematically misallocates across a session: orientation is worth far more at turn one than at turn forty, yet both are charged the same ceiling. Model the session as a sequential allocation problem - a total attention budget spread over an unknown number of remaining turns - and solve it with a simple index policy: spend proportionally more where the expected reduction in the agent's uncertainty is largest, which empirically means front-loading orientation, spending near-zero on repeat orientation when the delta is empty, and reserving a reserve tranche for the deep-context call that a hard problem eventually demands. This composes with (18) rather than competing with it: (18) decides how to degrade within one call once time or tokens run short, while (27) decides how much that call should have been allowed to spend in the first place. It also makes the delta projection work economically visible, because an empty delta should cost near nothing and free budget for later turns.\n\nSequencing: (25) is implementable immediately against the existing packer and is the cheapest measurable win; (24) needs the intent posterior that (19) already implies but does not expose; (26) is gated on the derived read index and the fleet-allocation primitive; (27) needs per-command budgets to exist first. None of the four requires the cognitive-memory ADR to be accepted - they are engine machinery, not memory subsystems, so no candidate items are filed on that ADR for them."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T12:33:08.547Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-25 (directed maintainer pass): Algorithm (19) model-conditioned packing profiles — the packer currently treats the consumer as a single abstract agent with one token budget, but the consumers differ in ways that are measurable and stable: context window size, tolerance for terse encodings, benefit from worked examples versus bare fields, and degradation curve as the prompt grows. Once identity carries the model (pm-03pq3o), the workspace can condition packing on the consumer instead of guessing. Mechanism: a profile is a vector of packing parameters — budget fraction reserved for graph neighbourhood versus item bodies, projection depth per candidate rank, redundancy penalty strength in the (4) submodular greedy, and the coarse-to-fine cut point of (9). Profiles are learned, never hardcoded per vendor: the usage-feedback ledger (pm-uwfs) already records which served candidates were subsequently touched, so per-model utilization is a direct observation, and the profile is the parameter set maximizing observed utilization per token for that model. Cold start uses the workspace-global profile, so a never-seen model is never worse than today. Falsifiability matters here: the mechanism is only worth shipping if per-model utilization differences exceed within-model variance, which is measurable before any implementation from existing usage data plus model provenance. Relation to existing entries: this conditions the parameters of (4) and (9) rather than adding a selection stage; it does not touch retrieval (7). Risk to bound: profiles must never become a channel that leaks one agent's behaviour into another's ranking beyond aggregate parameters."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T12:33:09.924Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-25 (directed maintainer pass): Algorithm (20) merge-probability-weighted provenance ranking — in a fleet working on branches, a workspace read on one branch sees facts of three kinds: mainline facts, this branch's own uncommitted facts, and facts that exist only in sibling branches once federation or merge preview makes them visible. Ranking them equally is wrong in both directions: an agent that trusts a sibling branch's not-yet-merged decision may build on work that never lands, while an agent that ignores its own branch's fresh decisions re-derives what it just decided. Mechanism: attach to each candidate fact a provenance weight derived from its position relative to the merge frontier — mainline facts weight 1, own-branch facts weight 1 for the authoring agent and decay for others, sibling-branch facts weight by an estimated merge probability. The estimate needs no prediction model: the history stream already carries the base rates that matter — how often branches by this author on this item class merge, how long the branch has diverged, whether it touches items already closed on mainline. Score contribution stays linear so the explanation surface can say a candidate was demoted because it exists only on an unmerged branch, which is the kind of explanation an agent can act on. Degenerate correctly: single-branch workspaces see weight 1 everywhere and identical behaviour to today. Relation: this is a signal feeding the (3) linear combination, not a new stage; it depends on merge-frontier visibility, so it is only actionable once cross-branch reads exist (pm-odohgb, pm-76dnfg)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T12:33:12.348Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-25 (directed maintainer pass): Algorithm (21) hash-keyed hierarchical summary pyramid — (9) chooses a coarse or fine representation of a candidate at read time, but at a million items the coarse representation of a large subtree must already exist, because summarizing an epic with four thousand descendants inside a sub-second read is not possible. Mechanism: maintain a summary per interior node of the parent hierarchy, keyed by a hash over the subtree's material state (child ids, their status, their updated versions). A read serves the stored summary when the key matches and marks it stale otherwise; refresh is incremental and bottom-up, touching only nodes whose subtree hash changed, which is the same fold shape as (6) write-time signal maintenance and should share its machinery. The pyramid gives the packer a genuine dial: represent a whole subtree in one summary row when the budget is tight, descend when it is not, with cost proportional to the depth served rather than the subtree size. Two properties must hold: a stale summary is always labelled rather than silently served, and the summary is derivable — losing the entire pyramid degrades performance and never correctness. Relation: write-side complement to (9); consumer of (6); the natural storage owner is the persistent derived index (pm-801d) rather than a separate artifact, and it should be filed there if the ADR accepts it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T12:33:13.616Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-25 (directed maintainer pass): Algorithm (22) counterfactual replay evaluation — every algorithm from (1) to (21) changes what pm serves, and the only honest question about such a change is whether an agent completed its task with fewer tokens and fewer wrong turns. Graded golden judgments (pm-atfm) answer whether the ranking matches an opinion; transcript replay (pm-8pnj) answers what a fixed transcript costs. Neither answers the counterfactual: given this recorded session, what would this agent have done had the scorer been different. Mechanism: record agent sessions as sequences of pm reads with the served candidate sets and the subsequent mutations, then replay a candidate scorer against the same request points and compare on three observable quantities — whether items the agent actually touched next were served at all, at what rank, and at what token cost. This is off-policy evaluation with the standard caveat stated honestly: it cannot capture what the agent would have done with items it never saw, so it bounds improvement rather than proving it, and a change that wins on replay still needs a live comparison before becoming default. The value is filtering: most scoring ideas can be rejected on recorded data in minutes instead of shipped and observed for weeks. Relation: evaluation infrastructure serving all of (1)-(21); consumes the usage ledger (pm-uwfs) and the judgment corpus (pm-lixy); owned by the evaluation harness (pm-atfm) rather than by any scoring item."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T17:55:26.153Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Algorithm (23) federated relevance calibration and cross-workspace budget allocation — extending the catalog 2026-07-25.\n\nGap this closes: algorithms (1)-(22) all assume a single workspace. Activation (1) is measured in that workspace's access rate, learned weights (3) are fit on its labels, sketches (8) summarize its history, and the shrinkage prior (21) pools within it. Federation (pm-odohgb, story pm-is1a) makes N workspaces addressable in one context assembly, and at that point every existing signal becomes incomparable across sources: a three-item scratch repo and a decade-old monolith produce activation values that differ by orders of magnitude for work of identical relevance. A naive merge lets the busiest workspace flood the budget, which is the failure mode an organization notices immediately.\n\nMethod, in three parts:\n\n1. Rank-normalize within source before any cross-source comparison. Convert each source's candidate scores to within-source quantiles, then fuse. This is the same insight that makes (5) reciprocal rank fusion the right cold-start combiner — rank is robust exactly where magnitude is not — applied one level up, from signals-within-a-workspace to workspaces-within-a-federation.\n\n2. Treat the token budget as an allocation across sources rather than a single pool. Each source receives budget proportional to its expected marginal value per token, estimated with the same submodular measure as (4), but with the redundancy penalty computed across sources as well as within them — an item mirrored or referenced in two federated workspaces must be paid for once. Sources with no local usage history back off to a federation-level shrinkage prior (21), so a newly joined workspace inherits the fleet's prior instead of a flat default and does not need a cold-start special case.\n\n3. Decay by sync lag, not wall clock. A remote workspace last synchronized three weeks ago has a recency distribution frozen at the sync point; scoring its items on wall-clock recency makes stale remotes look artificially hot. Activation from a remote source decays against its own last-sync watermark, and the watermark travels with the federated reference.\n\nDegeneracy requirement, per the catalog's standing rule: with one source the allocation reduces to (4) exactly and the normalization is the identity, so a single-workspace user pays nothing for federation-awareness.\n\nOwner: transport and addressing belong to pm-odohgb; the scoring changes land in the pm-h3no scorer contract once federated sources exist. Recorded on pm-g8t7b9."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T17:55:27.244Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Algorithm (24) fleet-joint context diversification — extending the catalog 2026-07-25.\n\nGap this closes: every algorithm in the catalog optimizes one agent's context in isolation. The workspace target is thousands of concurrent agents (pm-g2zz, pm-szv11n), and independently-optimal per-agent packing is jointly wasteful in two measurable ways. First, agents with similar tasks receive near-identical contexts, so the same tokens are paid N times across the fleet. Second, greedy relevance steers many agents toward the same few items, manufacturing exactly the collisions that work allocation (pm-3oqjnc) then has to resolve — the recommender creates the contention the scheduler must undo.\n\nMethod: lift the submodular greedy of (4) from per-agent to per-fleet. The objective becomes coverage of the relevant frontier by the union of all agents' contexts, subject to each agent's own token budget, with every agent's selection conditioned on what its peers already hold. The peer set does not require a coordinator — it is approximated from state the workspace already records: the claim ledger says who holds what, and a per-agent context sketch (8) summarizes what each has recently been served, both readable without new distributed machinery.\n\nFleet cache economics fall out of the same framing. If a thousand agents request context in the same minute, the honest implementation computes one shared scored frontier and derives per-agent deltas from it, rather than running a thousand independent scoring passes over the same candidate set. This composes with the write-time fold (6) and the funnel quotas (7): the fold maintains the shared frontier incrementally, the quotas bound its size, and per-agent work reduces to selection over an already-scored set.\n\nBoundary against pm-3oqjnc, stated so the two are never merged: allocation decides which agent works on which item; this decides what each agent sees given that assignment. They must read the same frontier or they will disagree about what is relevant, so the shared frontier is the interface between them, not a duplicated concept.\n\nDegeneracy requirement: with one agent the joint objective is exactly (4) and the peer-conditioning term vanishes. The small-end path (pm-z436) is unchanged, as the catalog requires."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T06:05:19.670Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, slug \"outcome-distance-prior\" — strategic reachability as a first-class relevance signal. Recorded 2026-07-26 with a slug rather than an ordinal, because the ordinal space collided three times on 2026-07-25 (see the registry finding on pm-g8t7b9); slugs are the collision-free form that registry now requires.\n\nGap this closes. Every signal in the catalog to date scores a candidate by its relationship to the AGENT: how recently it was touched (activation), what it is associated with (spreading activation, structural fingerprints), what is unfinished (tension), what changes with it (co-change coupling), what the current intent is (task-set and model conditioning), what peers are already covering (fleet diversification). Not one entry scores a candidate by its relationship to the PROJECT'S DECLARED OUTCOMES. Two items can be identically recent, identically associated, and identically unfinished, while one of them is a direct precondition of a shipping outcome and the other advances nothing anyone has committed to. The scorer currently cannot tell those apart, and an agent asked to pick work would like it to.\n\nWhy the signal is newly available rather than newly imagined. It was not computable before 2026-07-26. The strategic layer of this workspace was wired almost entirely with the untyped `related` kind: the six outcome milestones carried between zero and five inbound typed edges, so `pm graph impact <milestone> --kind implements,verifies --direction incoming` returned 0 to 5 nodes and there was no distance to read. After the typed-ladder pass the same query returns 16 to 123 nodes per milestone at shortest-path distances of 1 to 5, with 268 of 271 active items reaching at least one outcome. The signal exists now because the graph now carries the meaning; that ordering matters, because the algorithm is worthless on a graph wired with untyped edges and would have looked like a good idea anyway.\n\nMethod.\n\n1. Precompute, per outcome root, the shortest typed path length to every item over the semantic kind set (implements, verifies), in the write-time incremental fold that already maintains activation and sketches. This is a multi-source BFS over a sparse graph; on this workspace it costs one traversal of 4,877 edges and the result is a small integer per item per root.\n\n2. Convert distance to a prior with a decay that is scale-free rather than tuned: prior(i) = max over roots r of exp(-lambda * d(i,r)), with lambda derived from the observed distance distribution rather than hardcoded, so a shallow project and a deep one both get a usable spread. Items that reach no outcome get the floor value, not zero - unreachable is a filing gap, not evidence of irrelevance, and zeroing it would hide exactly the items a graph-hygiene pass wants to surface.\n\n3. Combine as a multiplicative prior on the existing linear score rather than as another additive term. The distinction is deliberate: outcome distance is not evidence that an item is relevant to the current task, it is evidence about how much the project cares. A multiplicative prior modulates the ranking the other signals produce without being able to promote an item the task-relevance signals rejected outright, which is the failure mode of adding strategic weight additively.\n\n4. Carry the path, not just the number, into the explanation. The rationale string an agent reads becomes \"2 hops from proven-scale via pm-9rxu\" rather than \"strategic prior 0.61\". This keeps the truthfulness rule the catalog imposes on the linear scorer: every contribution must be renderable as a reason a human would accept.\n\nInteraction with entries already in the catalog. Spreading activation propagates from what the agent is touching; this propagates from what the project has committed to. They are the two halves of the same graph read in opposite directions, and they should share one traversal rather than run two. The merge-probability provenance ranking entry conditions on which branch a fact came from; this conditions on which outcome a fact serves; both are edge-conditioned and both belong in the same edge-weighting pass. Fleet-joint diversification lifts packing from per-agent to per-fleet coverage of the relevant frontier - outcome distance is the natural definition of that frontier's boundary, so the two compose directly rather than competing.\n\nDegeneracy requirement, per the catalog's standing rule. In a workspace with no milestones and no typed semantic edges - the three-item scratch project of pm-z436 - every item is at floor distance, the prior is a constant, and the multiplicative term is the identity. The small end pays nothing, which is the same bar every other entry in this catalog has had to meet.\n\nRisk to record, because it is real and cuts against the entry. This signal rewards items that are well-wired and penalises items that are not, so it is only as honest as the graph's edge hygiene. A correctly-scoped item that nobody linked will rank below a marginal item that somebody linked well, and the scorer will look confidently wrong. Two existing items are the mitigation and this entry should not ship before them: pm-bzmeaa makes bounded outcome reachability an enforced invariant rather than an aspiration, so an unlinked item is a gate failure rather than a quietly demoted candidate; pm-4020c5 canonicalises the dependency-kind vocabulary, without which the semantic kind set is itself fragmenting and the traversal loses conductance to spelling drift. Recorded as a hard sequencing constraint rather than a caveat.\n\nOwner: the scorer contract (pm-h3no) for the combination step; the signal feature store (pm-3hps) for the precomputed distances, since it already owns write-time incremental maintenance of exactly this shape. Dispositioned into the portfolio registry (pm-g8t7b9) by slug, not filed as a standalone item, per the catalog's standing rule."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T09:57:47.693Z"
    author: "harness:opencode"
    text: "## Brainstorm 2026-07-26 — the context-algorithm stack, defined end to end\n\nGrounded in the live workspace (2,063 items, 4,920 edges) and composed with ADR pm-vvib (cognitive memory) and the registry pm-g8t7b9. Every algorithm below names its memory subsystem (per pm-vvib), its scale behavior, and its complexity class. Nothing in the query-time hot path may exceed O(result) against the derived index (pm-801d); anything O(N) belongs in derived-index construction, never in a read.\n\n### 1. Candidate generation — multi-channel retrieval (associative recall)\nFive recall channels, each bounded to k candidates from the index, unioned with dedupe:\n- **Graph channel:** bounded BFS over typed edges from the focus set (focus item, active claims, current plan step). Depth 1–2 for hot context; deeper only on explicit traversal. Edge-kind weights: blocked_by/implements > parent > verifies/discovered_from > related.\n- **Temporal channel:** recently-touched items from history-stream heads (working/episodic memory).\n- **Semantic channel:** existing hybrid search providers over item corpus with the agent's focus/query text.\n- **Structural channel:** ready-set (active leaves with no open blocked_by — today's compareCriticalItems behavior, preserved as a channel so consolidation never regresses the shipped behavior).\n- **Affinity channel:** items this PM_AUTHOR touched, claimed, or discussed (per-author episodic memory; per-harness via pm-gw6uyq).\n\n### 2. Scoring — multi-signal ranking with adaptive decay\nscore(item) = Σ wᵢ · normalize(sᵢ), weights versioned and replaceable via the SDK scorer contract (shipped pm-h3no), signal inputs from the feature store (shipped pm-3hps):\n- **Recency decay:** exponential, with half-life *adaptive to workspace age* — a minutes-old scratch project gets a half-life of minutes (everything is hot, pm-z436 spectrum); a decades-old workspace gets months (pm-5qmm epochs dominate). One formula, scale-invariant: half-life = clamp(workspaceMedianInterEventInterval × C).\n- **Graph proximity:** personalized PageRank from the focus set on the typed-edge graph; conductance-bounded so giant hubs (roadmap root) don't swamp the ranking.\n- **Criticality:** critical-path membership, betweenness/articulation, blast radius — reused from pm-7ob5 analytics; a blocker on the critical path outranks a leaf chore.\n- **Structured pressure:** priority, risk, severity, deadline distance (urgency = f(time-to-deadline, estimate)).\n- **Knowledge density:** learnings/decisions/evidence counts — past lessons re-injected (semantic memory), the anti-re-learning signal.\n- **Usage feedback:** served-then-acted-on outcomes (pm-uwfs) as a learned affinity correction; offline-evaluated only (pm-atfm gate), never free-drifting online weights.\n- **Staleness:** aging-WIP penalty; long-claimed items decay unless re-touched.\n\n### 3. Token-budgeted packing (working memory)\nGreedy marginal-utility-per-token knapsack: sort by score/estimated-token-cost, pack until budget (contract-declared, pm-5t33or); **degrade projections before dropping items** — full row → +deps → status+title → title-only. Graceful degradation is the forgetting curve (pm-vvib): never deletion, only not-loaded.\n\n### 4. Scale tiers — one algorithm family, four regimes\n| Regime | Items | Strategy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scratch | <100 | Exact scoring over all; zero config; reduces to today's ordering |\n| Standard | 100–10k | Index-assisted; all channels exact |\n| Scale | 10k–1M | Two-phase: cheap index filter to ~200 candidates → exact scoring; closed-epoch rollups (pm-5qmm) replace aged detail |\n| Civilizational | 1M+, decades | Epoch-tiered retrieval: era summaries first, drill-down on demand; per-era PageRank anchors; point-in-time scoring as-of T using only history ≤ T (pm-5gf0xf) |\n\n### 5. Explainability and determinism\nEvery ranked row can emit its score-breakdown vector (per-signal contributions) — `--explain-ranking`; same inputs ⇒ same ranking (seeded, no wall-clock dependence inside scoring). This is what makes context auditable and the pm-atfm judgments gradable.\n\n### 6. Fleet coordination algorithms (thousands of agents)\nRanking alone fails a fleet: 1,000 agents reading the same top-1 create a thundering herd on one item. The allocator (pm-3oqjnc) adds:\n- **Contention-aware assignment:** claimed/unclaimed-aware re-ranking; soft partitioning by graph communities (pm-7ob5) so agents spread across independent subgraphs.\n- **Critical-path-first:** fleet capacity goes to the longest-chain blockers first.\n- **Deterministic jitter:** hash(agent-id, item-id) tie-breaking — reproducible, no coordination server.\n- **Merge-safe convergence:** allocation decisions are ordinary mutations with history, so branch fleets merge under the field-aware driver (pm-g5sx, pm-2sbj8p).\n\n### 7. Learning loop (safe)\nOffline: pm-atfm golden judgments + ContextBench (pm-kw4b) score every weight/signal change (nDCG-style). Online: usage feedback (pm-uwfs) is captured as data, not applied until an eval gate passes. Weight versions are tracked items, so a context-quality regression is bisectable like code.\n\n### 8. Portfolio dispositions (feeds pm-g8t7b9)\nOwned here: channels 1–3, scale tiers, explainability. Owned by pm-7ob5: criticality analytics inputs. Owned by pm-3oqjnc: fleet allocation. Owned by pm-5qmm: consolidation. Owned by pm-uwfs: feedback signal. No algorithm lives only in this note — the registry row is the disposition of record."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T17:12:19.040Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, slug \"maintenance-invariant-temporal-coordinates\" — recorded 2026-07-26, extending the ledger by slug per the pm-g8t7b9 convention (never renumber historical entries).\n\n**Gap this closes.** Every temporal entry already in the catalog — the adaptive-half-life recency decay in the scoring section, (15) age-and-value-conditioned verbatim decay, (20) temporal non-stationarity mining, (22) lineage-aware relevance continuity — reasons about *when* an item was last touched and differs only in how that instant is discounted. All of them draw that instant from updated_at, which is the timestamp of the last write of any kind. None of them can distinguish an item that was advanced from an item that was swept. The catalog therefore has a shared blind spot rather than a missing entry, and no amount of better decay shape repairs it.\n\n**Why it is now load-bearing.** This is not a theoretical concern. Governance work the project mandates — resolution backfill, close-reason backfill, relationship enrichment, release attribution stamping, normalization, duplicate renames — writes updated_at on terminal items. The two backfills executed on 2026-07-26 rewrote updated_at on 1,782 and 1,713 items respectively, so the entire terminal corpus now presents as strictly newer than every genuinely active item. Recency carries weight 0.35 in the default vector, tied for the largest single weight. The engine's dominant temporal signal is therefore inverted with respect to truth immediately after exactly the maintenance the project requires. pm-07pt16 owns the defect; this entry owns the algorithm.\n\n**The algorithm.** Replace the single write-timestamp with a small vector of coordinates, each derived from data that already exists, and let each consuming signal choose the coordinate that matches its question.\n\n1. *Substantive-event time.* Classify every history operation as substantive (create, close, cancel, status transition, claim, release, comment, learning) or maintenance (dependency add, metadata backfill, normalization, redaction, repair, compaction). Substantive-event time is the ts of the most recent substantive entry. This is O(1) per item against the derived index because the classification is a property of the op field, already present on every entry, and can be folded into the stream head during index construction.\n2. *Release-cohort time.* For terminal items, the stamped release identifier gives a maintenance-invariant position on the delivery timeline, independent of every subsequent write. As of 2026-07-26 this is populated on 1,782 of 1,795 terminal items, the remainder being deliberately unstamped because they close after the newest tag.\n3. *Attention time.* The usage-feedback ledger (pm-uwfs) already records when context assembly last surfaced an item, which is when the workspace last *cared* about it — a different question from when it changed, and the right coordinate for habituation and for (13) salience-gated broadcast arbitration.\n4. *Structural time.* Position in the dependency graph relative to the current frontier, which is what (16) successor-representation predictive mapping actually wants and which no clock provides.\n\n**Composition rule.** Recency decay consumes substantive-event time with release-cohort time as its fallback and created_at as the floor. Habituation consumes attention time. Era segmentation in (20) consumes release-cohort time, which makes change-point detection immune to sweeps that would otherwise manufacture a spurious era boundary at every governance pass. Lineage continuity in (22) is unaffected because it already tracks identity flow rather than instants.\n\n**Degeneracy.** In a workspace with no history and no releases, every coordinate collapses to created_at and the ordering is exactly today's, so the small end (pm-z436) needs no special path. In a workspace with history but no releases, coordinates 1, 3 and 4 remain available and only the fallback is unused.\n\n**Falsifiable property.** Applying any pure-maintenance mutation to any item leaves the relevance ordering of the entire workspace unchanged. This is directly testable as a property and is the acceptance criterion carried on pm-07pt16."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T22:10:55.488Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, slug on-policy-context-evaluation-in-replayable-episodes, added 2026-07-27. Recorded by slug rather than by ordinal because the ledger already carries colliding numbers and the convention is that new entries do not renumber history.\n\nGap this closes. The catalog's evaluation entry, counterfactual replay evaluation, states its own limit honestly: it is off-policy, it cannot capture what an agent would have done with items it never saw, so it bounds improvement rather than proving it, and any scorer that wins on replay still needs a live comparison before it can become the default. That live comparison has no mechanism anywhere in the portfolio. Graded judgments compare a ranking against an opinion. Transcript replay prices a fixed transcript. Neither can rerun the agent.\n\nWhat makes it newly feasible. The episode substrate filed today makes a workspace constructible from a declared seed with an injectable clock and byte-identical reconstruction, and the environment story makes an agent's success on a task computable from recorded state rather than from self-report. Together those turn a context-scoring change into something that can be run rather than estimated: hold the task, the seed and the agent fixed, vary only the scorer, and observe completion, token spend and wrong turns on-policy.\n\nMechanism. Define an episode as a seeded workspace recipe, a task item, and a declared verdict computed from workspace state. For a scoring change, run N episodes under the incumbent scorer and N under the candidate, with the same seeds and the same agent, and compare on completion rate, tokens to completion, mutations that were later reverted, and reads that returned nothing the agent used. Because the seeds are shared, the comparison is paired, which is what makes small samples informative. Because the verdict is computed from recorded state, the grader has no per-episode code and cannot drift with the scorer under test. Because the episode is reconstructible, a regression is reproducible years later from the recipe alone.\n\nHonest limits, stated so this is not oversold. The agent under test is a specific model at a specific configuration, so results transfer across models only as far as the model-conditioned packing entry already assumes. Episodes are synthetic tasks and can be gamed by a scorer that overfits their shape, which is why the corpus must be drawn from real recorded sessions rather than authored. And running agents is orders of magnitude more expensive than replaying transcripts, so this is the confirmation stage of a funnel whose cheap stages remain the judgment corpus and the off-policy replay, never their replacement.\n\nDegeneracy. With one episode, one seed and no variance estimate it degenerates to a single before-and-after run, which is what an engineer does by hand today. With the agent held fixed and only recorded request points replayed it degenerates exactly to the existing off-policy entry, which is the intended relationship: same comparison, weaker evidence, far lower cost.\n\nRelation. Evaluation infrastructure serving the whole catalog, owned by the evaluation harness rather than by any scoring item, consuming the episode substrate and the environment story filed 2026-07-27 and the usage ledger."
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T04:16:41.976Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Extending the catalog 2026-07-27 — entry (28). The catalog runs (1)-(27) and is not restarted here.\n\n(28) Evidence half-life conditioned on cited-substrate churn — invalidation as a first-class signal, distinct from recency.\n\nThe problem the existing entries cannot reach. Every temporal entry in this catalog scores an item by facts about the item: (1) ACT-R base-level activation decays by time since the item's own accesses, (20) segments eras from change points in item activity, (22) tracks identity flow across copy, split, merge and supersede, and the staleness clause in the scoring notes penalises aging WIP. All of them answer how old the record is. None answers whether the record is still true. Those are different questions and in this workspace they routinely disagree. An item whose body carries a measurement of a specific file, a benchmark number, a line reference, or a byte count is a claim about a substrate that keeps moving after the claim is written. A finding recorded against a file that has since been rewritten sixty times is stale no matter how recently the item was touched, and a finding recorded against a file nobody has edited since is still exactly true no matter how old it is. Serving the first as fresh and burying the second as old is the failure mode, and it is invisible to every signal in (1)-(27) because they all read item metadata and never read the substrate.\n\nWhy this workspace can compute it and a generic tracker cannot. pm already stores the dependency between claim and substrate: items carry linked files, linked docs and linked tests, each with a path, and the repository carries the commit history of those paths. The join is therefore available without any new capture: for an item i with linked artifacts A(i), and an observation instant t(i) taken as the history instant at which the citing text was written rather than the item's updated_at, the churn exposure is the change activity over A(i) in the interval from t(i) to now. Concretely churn(i) = sum over a in A(i) of w(a) * c(a, t(i), now), where c is the number of commits touching path a in the window and w(a) weights how load-bearing the citation is, with a linked test weighted above a linked doc because a test asserts behaviour while a doc describes it. A missing or deleted path is the maximal case, not a zero: the substrate did not merely change, it left.\n\nThe scoring shape. Keep the linear, explainable form entry (3) fixes, and add one term rather than modifying (1). Trust(i) = exp(-churn(i) / H) where H is a fitted half-life in churn units, and the served relevance multiplies the existing score by Trust(i). This composes rather than competes: (1) still answers how recently the item mattered, (28) answers how much of what it says can still be relied on, and an item can be simultaneously recent and untrustworthy or old and solid, which is precisely the distinction the current model collapses. The uncertainty machinery in (21) applies unchanged because H is a pooled estimate with a small-sample regime, and the write-time fold in (6) is where churn accumulates: a commit touching path a increments a per-path counter, so no read ever walks the repository log.\n\nWhat it changes for the consumer. Three behaviours follow directly. First, the evidence-bearing items an agent is most likely to act on are ranked by whether their evidence survived, so a measured claim from three weeks ago about an untouched file outranks a restated claim from yesterday about a file under active rewrite. Second, the ranking explanation gains a term an agent can act on: not merely that an item scored low, but that it cites artifacts which have moved N times since the claim was written, which is an instruction to re-measure rather than to ignore. Third, it gives the corpus a maintenance signal it currently lacks — the set of items whose trust has decayed below a threshold is exactly the set whose findings should be re-verified before being cited, and today nothing in the system can name that set.\n\nBoundaries against neighbouring entries, so this is not a re-spelling. (12) co-change coupling mining measures which artifacts move together in order to expand a candidate set; (28) measures how much one artifact moved after a specific claim in order to discount that claim, and is a per-item scalar rather than a pairwise association. (14) retrieval-conditioned contradiction gating fires when two retrieved items assert incompatible things; (28) fires with a single item and no contradiction present, because substrate motion is evidence of possible staleness without any second claim. (15) age-and-value-conditioned verbatim decay decides which bytes to compact away; (28) decides how much to trust bytes that are fully retained. (20) detects regime changes in item activity; (28) reads repository activity, which is a different series and moves when the tracker is idle.\n\nPrerequisites and placement. The write-time fold (6) owns the per-path counter, the linked-artifact join is already public through the annotation and link primitives, and the fitted half-life H is a pm-atfm evaluation question rather than a free parameter. Nothing here requires a new capture surface, which is why it is proposed as an engine entry rather than as a memory subsystem candidate under the cognitive-memory ADR."
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T12:56:49.162Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, slug ordering-slack-conditioned-urgency, recorded 2026-07-27 by slug per the pm-g8t7b9 ordinal-collision rule. The catalog runs (1)-(28) and is not restarted here.\n\nGap this closes. Every scoring signal in the catalog answers a question about an item in isolation or about its similarity to the request: recency, affinity, structural position, outcome distance, evidence half-life. None answers the scheduling question, which is what it costs the project if this particular item waits. Two items can be equally relevant to what an agent is doing and have completely different consequences for delay: one sits on the only path to a dated milestone and every day it waits moves the milestone, the other has six weeks of float. Relevance treats them identically today. The outcome-distance-prior entry is the closest neighbour and is explicitly not this: it measures how far an item is from an outcome, which is a distance in the reachability graph, whereas this measures how much freedom the schedule has to defer it, which is a quantity in the ordering graph.\n\nThe algorithm. Over the ordering DAG, compute for each node the standard critical-path quantities: earliest start from predecessors, latest start from the deadline of every outcome it can reach, and slack as their difference. Convert slack to an urgency multiplier that is monotone decreasing and saturating, so zero-slack work is strongly promoted, moderate float is neutral, and very large float does not become a penalty that buries legitimate exploratory work. Multiply into the existing composite score rather than adding, so it modulates relevance instead of competing with it. Condition the multiplier on the requester: an agent working toward a specific target uses slack computed against that target's deadline, and an agent with no declared target uses the workspace-wide minimum over reachable outcomes.\n\nDegeneracy, which is the acceptance condition every catalog entry has to state. In a workspace with no ordering edges, every node has infinite slack, the multiplier saturates to one everywhere, and the score is byte-identical to the score without this entry. That is the correct behaviour and it is also this workspace today: 664 of 9,502 edges carry sequence and 1,765 of 2,007 ordered nodes have no predecessor, so the entry would currently be a no-op and must be shown to be one. In a workspace with no deadlines the same thing happens through a different route, because latest-start is undefined and the entry declines rather than inventing a horizon.\n\nCost and scale. Slack is one forward and one backward topological pass, linear in edges, and it is recomputed only when the ordering graph or a deadline changes, which is far less often than items change. At million-item scale the passes run over the ordering projection alone, which is a small fraction of all edges, and the per-request cost is a table lookup. The small end degenerates as described rather than through a special-cased branch.\n\nDependencies and honesty constraint. This entry is unusable until the ordering graph is non-degenerate, so it is gated on pm-gxs55a rather than on any packer or store work, and it must never be allowed to create pressure to manufacture ordering edges in order to make itself useful. The 2026-07-27 extraction tranche under pm-ye46ns found ten honest precedence statements in the whole corpus, which is the measure of how far away that is. Forecast distributions from pm-uey8od are the natural upgrade: slack computed against a probabilistic completion date rather than a point deadline, with the multiplier taking the probability of the item becoming critical rather than a binary."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T15:06:04.775Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, slug recorded-order-arbitration, added 2026-07-28 by slug per the numbering rule on pm-g8t7b9. The catalog runs (1) through (28) plus the slug entries and is not restarted.\n\nGap this closes. Every scoring signal in the catalog that uses the ordering graph — (11) open-loop tension, the ordering-slack-conditioned-urgency entry, (16) successor-representation prediction, outcome-distance-prior — treats an ordering edge as an authoritative statement about direction. This pass proved that assumption false in the live corpus. The direction of an ordering edge is a function of which spelling the author used and which endpoint they wrote it from: 219 rows say blocks and 127 say blocked_by, the two are resolved asymmetrically, and inside the v1 milestone chain the same kind is used in two opposite senses within single items. Any urgency or slack computed over that graph is computed over a mixture of correct and inverted edges with no way to tell them apart, and the mixture is not small.\n\nThe algorithm. Treat the direction of an ordering edge as a quantity to be inferred rather than read, using the immutable record as the arbiter, and expose the inference so downstream scoring can discount what it cannot establish.\n\nStep one, the declared reading. Canonicalize every accepted ordering spelling into a single relation and record the spelling it came from and the endpoint that authored it. This is the write-path decision pm-4020c5 owns; the algorithm consumes its output and does not substitute for it.\n\nStep two, the observed reading. For each ordering pair, derive the direction actually exhibited by the history streams. The relevant observable is not the edge's own created_at, which records when someone wrote the claim, but the terminal entries of the two endpoints: which item's close or cancel entry precedes the other's, and whether the successor's first substantive entry follows the predecessor's terminal entry. Both are hash-chained and cannot be edited after the fact, which is what makes them a usable arbiter rather than another claim. Items that never closed contribute no observation and must not be forced to.\n\nStep three, agreement and confidence. Each pair lands in one of four states: agreed, where declared and observed match; contested, where they are opposite; unobservable, where the record cannot speak because one endpoint is still active or has no terminal entry; and unconstrained, where no ordering was declared and the observation is coincidence rather than dependency. The last distinction is the one that keeps this honest — temporal succession is not evidence of dependency, so an observed order with no declared edge produces a candidate for review, never an edge.\n\nStep four, use. Scoring consumers read a confidence rather than a boolean. Agreed edges carry full weight. Contested edges are weighted down and, critically, are surfaced rather than silently corrected: a contested edge is a governance finding for pm-6x7o and a disposition for the owner, not something a relevance engine repairs. Unobservable edges keep the declared direction at reduced weight, which is the correct default because most active work has no terminal evidence yet. Slack, tension and reachability then degrade gracefully on a corpus whose ordering is partly wrong instead of confidently misranking it.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, both required. On a workspace with no terminal items — the first minutes of a new tracker, pm-z436's small end — every pair is unobservable, every edge keeps its declared direction at the reduced weight uniformly, and the ranking is therefore identical to the current declared-only behaviour up to a constant factor. On a corpus whose declared and observed orders agree everywhere, every confidence is one and the algorithm is a no-op. Neither degenerate case may take a special code path; both must fall out of the same computation, which is the acceptance property that distinguishes this from a heuristic.\n\nCost. The observation is a per-pair lookup of two terminal timestamps, so it is linear in edges and needs only the two entries per stream that the point-read path already addresses, not a stream replay. It is cacheable on the same fingerprint the graph index already uses and invalidates only when an endpoint reaches a terminal status, which is rare relative to reads. At the million-item scale pm-o87av6 targets this is a batch derivation with an incremental update on close, not a per-query cost.\n\nRelationship to existing entries, so this is not folded into them by mistake. (22) lineage-aware relevance continuity tracks mass flowing across copy, split and supersede events and assumes the edges it follows are correctly directed; this supplies that assumption. The ordering-slack-conditioned-urgency entry consumes slack and must not compute it over unweighted edges once this exists. (20) temporal non-stationarity mines eras from change points in activity and is orthogonal: it asks when the project's behaviour changed, this asks whether a specific recorded ordering is true. Federated calibration in (23) is cross-workspace and inherits the confidence rather than defining it.\n\nOwner note: this entry is machinery for the relevance engine and does not become a pm-vvib memory candidate. The prerequisite decision is pm-4020c5, the first consumer is the ordering-slack entry, and the governance surface for contested pairs is pm-6x7o."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T22:37:05.155Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, slug refuted-and-abandoned-evidence-retrieval, added 2026-07-28 by slug per the pm-g8t7b9 ordinal-collision rule. The catalog runs (1)-(28) plus the slugged entries outcome-distance-prior, maintenance-invariant-temporal-coordinates, on-policy-context-evaluation-in-replayable-episodes, ordering-slack-conditioned-urgency and recorded-order-arbitration, and is extended here rather than restarted.\n\nTHE SIGNAL CLASS THAT IS MISSING. Every scoring dimension in the catalog so far ranks work that exists and mostly work that is open. The corpus also records, permanently, what was tried and stopped: 13 canceled items, every tombstoned id whose stream survives its item, every close whose resolution says the approach was wrong rather than that it shipped, and - the most expensive category - the refusals recorded in prose when a pass deliberately declined to act. The ordering-extraction pass is the clearest example: 30 candidate edges were evaluated, 10 applied and 20 dropped, a 67 percent refusal rate, with three named drop classes. That refusal is the single most valuable retrieval target in the whole corpus for any agent about to attempt the same extraction, and no scoring dimension can reach it, because it is not an item, not a status, and not an edge - it is an assertion inside a note on an item that is otherwise about something else.\n\nThe failure mode is concrete and recurring. An agent orients, sees no open item for a plausible improvement, and re-derives a conclusion the project already paid for. The negative result is in the record and invisible to the ranker. At fleet scale this is the dominant duplicated cost, because every agent independently rediscovers the same dead ends, and the project's own governance rule - never create duplicated items - is enforced against titles rather than against conclusions.\n\nTHE ALGORITHM. Three stages, each degenerate-safe.\n\nStage one, extraction. Mine the immutable record for refusal and abandonment assertions and materialise them as first-class retrievable units with their own identity, rather than leaving them as substrings. Sources in descending reliability: a canceled status with a close reason, which is explicit and needs no inference; a resolution field describing a rejected approach; a recorded op that reverses a prior op on the same field within one stream, which is abandonment expressed as a mutation pattern and needs no language at all; and finally prose refusals in notes and comments, which need extraction and must carry a confidence below the structured sources. Each unit records what was attempted, what was declined, the stated reason, the item it was recorded on, and the recorded timestamp - so it inherits the same provenance the rest of the engine scores over.\n\nStage two, matching. Score a candidate action against the refusal set. The naive form is lexical and will fail, because the same dead end is described in different words each time. The available structural forms are stronger and already exist in the engine: the structural fingerprint of (10) matches a candidate against the relational shape of prior attempts, and the co-change coupling of (12) matches against the artifacts a prior attempt touched. A candidate that is structurally isomorphic to a recorded refusal over the same artifacts is the high-confidence case; a candidate that merely shares vocabulary is the low-confidence case and must be surfaced with that distinction visible, never collapsed into one score.\n\nStage three, injection with an explicit polarity. A matched refusal enters the assembly as a distinct, labelled element - not as a ranked item competing for the same slots as actionable work. This matters for two reasons. Actionability ranking would either bury it, since a canceled item is correctly not actionable, or promote it wrongly, since a fresh refusal looks recent. The polarity must be part of the payload so a consumer can tell \"here is work to do\" from \"here is what was tried and declined and why\", and the budget must reserve a bounded slice for the second, because a refusal that only appears when there is room left is a refusal that appears exactly when nobody needs it.\n\nTHE DECAY QUESTION, and why it inverts. Every other freshness dimension in the engine decays: older evidence matters less. Refusals partly invert this. A refusal grounded in an invariant - the direction of a stored relation, a format constraint, a policy - does not weaken with age and should decay slowly or not at all. A refusal grounded in a substrate that has since changed is worse than useless, because it will suppress work that is now correct. The catalog already holds the instrument for this distinction in (28), substrate-churn evidence half-life, and this entry is its first consumer with a concrete polarity: churn shortens a refusal's half-life, invariance extends it, and a refusal whose cited substrate has demonstrably changed must be surfaced as stale rather than silently dropped, so the agent learns that the question is open again.\n\nDEGENERACY, which is the acceptance condition. On a workspace with no canceled items, no reversing mutations, and no recorded refusals, this entry must contribute exactly nothing and cost nothing measurable - the extraction finds an empty set and the reserved budget slice returns to the general pool. On a single-item workspace it is trivially inert. That makes it safe to enable by default, which matters, because a signal an agent must opt into is a signal that will not be there the one time it was needed.\n\nRELATION TO NEIGHBOURS, none of which cover this. (11) open-loop tension scores unresolved loops and is the opposite polarity - it ranks what is still open, this ranks what was deliberately closed without success. (14) retrieval-conditioned contradiction gating resolves a conflict between retrieved memory and new evidence at read time; this retrieves the negative evidence in the first place, and (14) is what should arbitrate when a refusal contradicts a live item. (15) age-and-value-conditioned decay governs verbatim retention and is the mechanism this entry's inverted half-life rides on. (22) lineage-aware relevance continuity follows identity through supersedes and rename; a refusal has no successor by definition, which is precisely why lineage cannot reach it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T22:37:06.038Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, slug trust-weighted-context-assembly, added 2026-07-28 by slug per the pm-g8t7b9 ordinal-collision rule. Extends the catalog; renumbers nothing.\n\nTHE ASSUMPTION EVERY EXISTING ENTRY MAKES. Entries (1) through (28) score recorded evidence on relevance, recency, structure, outcome distance, and cost. Not one of them scores the WRITER. Every signal is treated as equally credible regardless of which agent, harness, model, or effort level produced it, and regardless of whether that writer's prior contributions turned out to be correct. On a single-author workspace that assumption is free. On a fleet of a thousand agents of mixed capability it is the load-bearing weakness of the whole engine, because context assembled from a confidently wrong writer is indistinguishable from context assembled from a careful one, and the assembly is what the next agent acts on. Roadmap horizon I states the general problem - correctness when part of the fleet is wrong - and this entry is the relevance-engine half of it.\n\nWHAT THE RECORD ALREADY SUPPORTS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. The provenance now present on history entries is a real substrate: author with an author_source distinguishing detected from asserted, agent_harness, agent_instance which is live on 5,108 entries, and agent_provenance carrying effort as a structured value with its own source - verified live as effort xhigh from environment. That is enough to group evidence by writer and by writer class. Three things are missing and each bounds what this entry can compute. Model is null on all 40,353 entries because the primary harness publishes no model environment key at all, owned by pm-ffz0a9. Session topic and role are carried by zero entries and must be supplied rather than detected, owned by pm-itsjf0. And identity itself is not unique under the mandated automatic detection, since every process of one harness collapses to the same author string, owned by pm-z8qd4k. So trust can be computed per harness and per instance today, per model when ffz0a9 lands, and per principal only when identity is verifiable rather than merely detected.\n\nTHE ALGORITHM. A per-writer credibility posterior, consumed as a scoring multiplier and as an assembly-composition constraint.\n\nEstimation. Treat each writer as a unit with a Beta-distributed reliability whose evidence is its own recorded outcomes: an assertion later contradicted by a recorded correction, a filed defect later closed as not-a-defect, an edge later retired, a refusal later overturned. These are all observable in the immutable record without any human labelling, which is what makes the estimate maintainable. Shrink aggressively toward the writer's class prior - harness, then model, then effort tier - using the hierarchical empirical-Bayes machinery of (21), because most writers will have too few observations to estimate individually and an unshrunk estimate on three observations is noise wearing a number.\n\nApplication, in two distinct ways that must not be conflated. As a multiplier, credibility scales an item's or assertion's contribution to the relevance score, bounded so that no writer can be scored to zero - a low-trust writer's evidence is discounted, never censored, because censorship makes the corpus unverifiable and hides the disagreement that (14) exists to arbitrate. As a composition constraint, an assembly must not be sourced predominantly from one low-trust writer even when that writer's items score highest: the assembly declares its trust profile and the packer diversifies across writers when the profile is thin. This is where the entry composes with (24) fleet-joint diversification, which diversifies across agents to reduce redundant work; here diversification serves correctness rather than coverage, and the two objectives can disagree - a case the packer must resolve explicitly rather than by accident of ordering.\n\nDisclosure, which is not optional. Any assembly whose scores were modified by trust must say so, name the dimension, and remain reproducible. pm-8bgeye already requires an assembly to record the inputs that produced it; a credibility posterior is such an input and must appear in the attestation, or the same request will produce different answers as posteriors move with no way to explain the difference. An undisclosed trust weight is indistinguishable from a ranking bug.\n\nRetroactive revision, the hardest property and the one that makes this worth designing early. When a writer's credibility falls, every assembly that was built on its evidence is retrospectively suspect. The record is immutable and must stay so, which means revision cannot rewrite anything - it must be expressible as a later assertion that reweights the past without altering it. Horizon I names this as retroactive quarantine and graded trust. The design constraint this entry contributes is that quarantine must be a recorded, reversible assertion carrying its own provenance, so a trust decision is itself auditable and a wrongly quarantined writer can be restored without erasing the quarantine.\n\nDEGENERACY. On a single-writer workspace every posterior equals the class prior, every multiplier is the same constant, the composition constraint is vacuous, and the entry must be provably a no-op - identical output, no measurable cost. That is the acceptance test, and it is the same test (23) and (24) carry, for the same reason: a fleet feature that changes single-agent behaviour is a regression disguised as a capability.\n\nBOUNDARIES. (20) merge-probability-weighted provenance ranking weights evidence by how likely it is to survive a merge, which is about durability, not credibility - evidence certain to merge can still be wrong. pm-u14c owns per-entry authenticity, which is whether a writer is who it claims; trust presumes authenticity and cannot substitute for it. pm-3oqjnc owns routing work to capable agents, which acts before the work; this acts on what they wrote afterwards. pm-2vs1za owns comparing cost to forecast value, and a credibility posterior is one of its inputs rather than a competitor to it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T04:53:05.081Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Planning pass 2026-07-29 (directed maintainer pass). Three catalog entries, by slug, each checked against the 42 notes already here and each carrying its degenerate case.\n\nEntry slug omission-cost-asymmetric-ranking. Purpose: rank candidates by the expected regret of leaving them out, not by the probability that they are relevant. Every scorer in this catalog optimises a value-per-token quantity in which a candidate's contribution is symmetric - including a marginal item costs tokens, excluding it costs the value it would have carried. That symmetry is false for one important class. A Decision that forbids the approach an agent is about to take has low lexical similarity to the task, low recency, and possibly no edge to the work at hand, so every existing signal ranks it out; and omitting it costs a wrong implementation, a review cycle, and a revert. Mechanism. Attach to each candidate an omission-loss term L_omit(i) that is learned rather than declared, estimated from the record: the fraction of past episodes in which the item was NOT served, and the retrieving agent's subsequent work was later superseded, reverted, reopened, or became the source of a discovered_from edge pointing back at the item. All four of those are already typed edges or recorded status transitions, so the label is derivable from immutable history with no new stored state, exactly as the learning-to-rank entry derives its pairwise labels. Combine as score(i) = base_value(i) + lambda * P(task class matches the item's constraint scope) * L_omit(i), keeping the whole expression linear so explain-ranking stays truthful. Constraint-bearing types - accepted Decisions, open ADRs, items whose acceptance criteria contain a prohibition - form the prior class that starts with a nonzero L_omit before any evidence accumulates, because the cold-start cost of learning this from scratch is paid in exactly the incidents it exists to prevent. Distinction from neighbours. Value-per-token and marginal-utility-per-token price inclusion; this prices exclusion, and the two differ only when the loss is asymmetric, which is the whole point. Outcome-distance-prior asks how close an item is to a goal; this asks what breaks if the agent never sees it. Verify-before-serve gates whether a served fact is trustworthy; this gates whether an untrusted-but-critical fact should be served anyway. Degenerate case that must be proved as a no-op: with L_omit uniform across all candidates the added term is a constant and the ranking is bit-identical to the existing marginal-utility ordering. A workspace with no supersedes, no reopen and no discovered_from edges therefore behaves exactly as today, which also means a brand-new workspace pays nothing.\n\nEntry slug absence-as-signal-coverage-complement. Purpose: report what the served context does not cover, over a declared partition, so the agent learns the shape of its own blind spot in the same call. Today a context or search answer is a ranked list and its silence is indistinguishable from absence of subject matter. Mechanism. Declare a coverage partition - the natural ones already exist in this product: the files graph, the docs graph, the type and epic partition, and the area tags. For a request, compute the intersection of the task's declared scope with the partition, subtract the union of the partition cells covered by the served set, and report the residual cells ranked by intersection weight times unclaimedness, where unclaimedness is how few active items reference the cell at all. Emit at most k residual cells with their weight, never their contents, so the cost is bounded by k rather than by corpus size. The output answers the question that the mandate to track everything actually poses: you are editing this file and no open item owns it; this epic has eleven children and none of them is reachable from the work you are doing. Distinction from neighbours. Learning-progress-guided gap scheduling ranks what the workspace should learn next as an exploration queue over epistemic value; this reports, per request, which declared cells the current answer failed to touch, and it is silent when coverage is complete. Retrieval-without-outcome measures served items that led nowhere; this measures cells that were never served at all. Inhibition-of-return suppresses what has already been shown; this surfaces what has never been shown. Degenerate case: when the served set covers every intersecting cell the residual is empty and the receipt is one empty field, so a small workspace where every file is owned by an item pays a constant. When no partition is declared, the entry is inert.\n\nEntry slug pinned-epoch-context-determinism. Purpose: make a context answer reproducible and attributable when thousands of agents are writing concurrently. Today assembly reads whatever the workspace happens to contain at wall-clock time T. Two agents asking the same question a second apart get different answers with no way to tell whether the difference came from their questions or from an unrelated write, and an agent cannot later reconstruct the context that justified its own action. Mechanism. Bind assembly to a pinned logical epoch rather than to now: a monotone workspace-level counter over committed mutations, which is the branch-independent ordering identity that pm-10l1vm establishes and which does not exist yet - so this entry is explicitly downstream of it. An agent either pins the epoch it read at, or accepts the current epoch and is told which one it got. Three properties follow. First, determinism: two assemblies at the same epoch with the same request produce byte-identical output, which makes context assembly testable as a pure function and makes an eval harness meaningful rather than approximate. Second, attributability: an action can cite the epoch its context came from, and workspace-level point-in-time projection replays exactly that context, which is what turns the served-then-acted-on signal from a correlation into a reconstruction. Third, cache correctness: an epoch-indexed cache key is monotone and never needs invalidation reasoning, which dissolves the single-slot mutual-eviction failure recorded on pm-77okxr rather than patching it, because two readers at different epochs are asking for genuinely different objects and can both be cached. Cost. The epoch counter is one integer folded at commit; assembly gains a bound rather than a scan; caching improves. The hard part is not cost but that pinning must degrade gracefully when an epoch has been compacted away, in which case assembly must report the nearest retained epoch rather than silently answering at now. Degenerate case: a single agent with no concurrent writers advances the epoch only through its own mutations, so every read pins the epoch its last write produced and the behaviour is identical to reading now. A workspace with one item and no history reads at epoch zero forever.\n\nOwner mapping for all three is recorded on pm-g8t7b9 in the same pass. None of the three requires new stored state beyond what the immutable streams and the existing fold already hold, which keeps them inside the no-daemon constraint of ADR pm-i7i4."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T16:59:47.781Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog extension 2026-07-29, two entries by slug. The catalog is not restarted and no historical numbering is changed.\n\nEntry slug action-legality-conditioned-assembly.\n\nThe gap. Every scoring signal in the engine ranks items - what is relevant, recent, blocking, close to an outcome, likely to be touched. Nothing in the assembly says what the reader may legally do next. An agent receives a bounded set of items and must then discover, one failed mutation at a time, which transitions those items admit. Measured this session: the workspace schema declares six role-to-status aliases and no transition relation at all, so a closed item accepts a move to in_progress or even to draft with no warning, and there is no machine-readable source from which the legal action set for an item in a state could be read.\n\nThe mechanism. Condition the assembly on the action space, not only the item space. For each candidate, carry the set of transitions and mutations that are legal for the reading identity given the item's current state, and let that set participate in scoring: an item whose only legal action is to wait is worth fewer tokens than one that can be started now, even at equal relevance. Two consequences follow. A context assembly becomes a policy input rather than a reading list, which is what an evaluation episode or a reinforcement-learning policy needs. And the omission cost changes shape - withholding an item the reader cannot act on is cheap, withholding the one item they could have started is the expensive mistake.\n\nDegeneracy requirement. When the transition relation is total and unrestricted - every action legal for every item, which is the product's behaviour today - every candidate carries an identical action set, the term contributes a constant, and ranking must be bit-identical to the current engine. That is the acceptance test, and it is why this entry cannot land before a declared transition relation exists.\n\nPrerequisites and owners. The declarative policy engine owns the transition relation as data. The episode substrate is the first consumer that needs the action set to be observable. The relevance contract and the token-budget packer own the scoring surface this term plugs into.\n\nEntry slug shape-conditioned-parameter-selection.\n\nThe gap. The engine's constants - recency half-life, decay rates, the token budgets, the candidate caps, the sample bounds - were all chosen while looking at one corpus of roughly two thousand items spanning seven months. Roadmap horizon Q records that the product cannot say which of its fitted numbers travel to another population. The instrument to answer it did not exist either: the only synthetic corpus generator varies size and nothing else, so at a million items the fixture is eleven simulated days deep with exactly one history entry per item, against a real mean of 18.7 entries per stream. That is now tracked as its own item.\n\nThe mechanism. Make the parameters a function of the corpus's measured shape rather than compiled-in scalars. Measure the population once and cache it - age span and density, history entries per item, hierarchy depth and fan-out distribution, edge-kind mix, annotation volume, author cardinality, arrival rate - then select each constant from that profile through a declared, published rule. A half-life expressed as a fraction of the corpus's observed activity span behaves correctly on a workspace minutes old and on an archive spanning decades, where a fixed number of days is wrong at both ends. A candidate cap expressed as a function of measured branching factor stops sampling three percent of a wide corpus while reading a narrow one exhaustively.\n\nDegeneracy requirement. On a corpus whose measured shape matches this repository's, every selected value must equal the constant it replaces, to the last digit. The proof is a two-part test: exact reproduction on this shape, and a materially different selection on a deliberately different shape, with the difference explained by the rule rather than by tuning.\n\nWhy it is an engine entry and not only a governance chore. The inventory of constants and their declared status is governance and is owned elsewhere. What belongs here is the ranking consequence: a scoring function whose parameters are read from the population makes the small-project and multi-decade ends of the spectrum the same code path instead of two special cases, which is what the small-end scale item and the multi-decade readability item both need and neither can get from a fixed constant.\n\nPrerequisites and owners. Shape-capable corpus generation is the instrument. The constants inventory is the input. The relevance contract owns the published rule. Hierarchical shrinkage, already in the catalog, is the natural companion: it supplies calibrated uncertainty for the estimates this entry would condition on, and the two should be designed together rather than sequentially."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T21:26:12.075Z"
    author: "harness:codex"
    text: "Context algorithm architecture\n\nCandidate generation is multi-channel and bounded: explicit query/filter matches; lifecycle-ready work; dependency frontier and dominator impact; recent substantive history deltas; semantic retrieval; active claim/session neighborhood; deadline, incident, release, and risk signals; and diversity candidates from underrepresented components. Each channel has a quota and an index cursor so no channel can force a full scan.\n\nFeature families are versioned SDK data, not CLI constants: actionability, urgency, dependency impact, milestone reachability, substantive recency distinct from governance-write recency, semantic similarity, claim affinity, risk and confidence, evidence density, novelty, recurrence, verification freshness, and uncertainty/provenance quality. Raw features, normalization corpus, weights, and missing-value behavior are explainable.\n\nRanking is constrained optimization under an explicit token budget. A deterministic scorer produces utility and uncertainty. A diversity-aware submodular or maximal-marginal-relevance selector packs the highest marginal context value per token while preserving dependency closure, critical blockers, failure evidence, and component coverage. Projection degrades from full to compact to brief before dropping a selected item. Omission receipts state every loss.\n\nScale strategy: bitmap and ordered secondary indexes for filters; an append-driven context-signal store for derived features; incremental dynamic-graph summaries for reachability, dominators, communities, and critical paths; approximate nearest-neighbor retrieval for semantics; sketches for distributions; content-addressed shared assemblies for fleet reuse; cursor-stamped invalidation after authoritative mutations. Tiny workspaces bypass expensive indexes and return the complete truthful answer.\n\nEvaluation is multi-objective: relevance judgments, recall of the eventual acted-on item, NDCG and reciprocal rank, dependency completeness, calibration of confidence, regret against an oracle replay, tokens per useful fact, latency, memory, cache reuse, and cross-corpus stability. Negative controls must prove that a ranking gate fails when relevance is deliberately inverted, evidence is stale, or explanations exceed the answer budget."
    edited_at: "2026-07-29T21:27:50.329Z"
  - created_at: "2026-07-30T09:24:36.574Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, by slug: cross-channel-disagreement-salience\n\nMechanism. The engine already scores candidates by metadata, graph position, history recency and usage affinity. This entry adds a signal that is not a property of any single item: the degree to which two independent recording channels that should agree about the same fact do not. Where they disagree, the disagreement itself is the highest-value context an agent can receive, because a divergence is exactly the place where the agent's model of the project is most likely to be wrong, and it is the one thing no single-channel read can reveal.\n\nChannels the corpus already carries, all readable today. Item field versus history entry for the same field. Declared contract versus rendered help. Declared token budget versus measured payload. Item status versus the readiness the edge set implies. A relation written from one endpoint versus the same relation read from the other. Close metadata versus the release the changelog attributes. Each pair has a defined agreement predicate, so disagreement is computable rather than judged.\n\nScoring. For each candidate, compute a disagreement mass over the channel pairs that reference it: the count of violated agreement predicates weighted by how load-bearing each channel is for the current intent. Add that mass as an additive term to the existing relevance score, and attach the violated predicate to the candidate as its explanation, so a disagreement never enters the context window without saying what disagrees with what. Cap the term so a corpus full of legacy divergence cannot crowd out ordinary work - disagreement is a salience boost, never a sort key.\n\nRequired degeneracies, which are the acceptance conditions. On a corpus with one recording channel the term is identically zero and assembly is byte-identical to today. On a corpus where every predicate holds the term is identically zero. On a small workspace of a few items and a few minutes the term is zero because no divergence has had time to accrue, so the small end pays nothing for the machinery. Under a token budget the disagreement explanations degrade before the disagreements themselves, so the agent learns that something disagrees even when it cannot afford to learn what.\n\nWhy this belongs in the engine and not only in a gate. A gate can fail a build on divergence, but a gate runs on a schedule and reports to a human. An agent assembling context mid-task needs the divergence surfaced in the payload it is already paying for, ranked against the work it was going to do anyway. The whole method that produced this session's findings was manual cross-channel comparison; this entry is that method moved inside the assembler.\n\nInteraction with existing entries. It composes with trust-weighted-context-assembly, which weights sources by reliability - here a channel that repeatedly loses a disagreement is exactly a channel whose trust weight should fall, so the two entries feed each other. It is distinct from refuted-and-abandoned-evidence-retrieval, which retrieves conclusions already rejected; a disagreement is unresolved, not refuted. It is distinct from recorded-order-arbitration, which decides which of two recorded orders to believe; this entry deliberately refuses to decide and reports the conflict."
  - created_at: "2026-07-30T17:12:42.066Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, by slug: budget-feasible-projection-descent\n\nRecorded 2026-07-30 (evening). Extends the catalog; no prior entry is renumbered or restated. Owner recorded on the algorithm portfolio registry.\n\nThe gap this closes. The packer already selects a diverse candidate set under a token budget, and the intent layer already enforces a declared ceiling at egress. Between them there is no algorithm that answers the only question that matters when the two disagree: given a budget too small for the projection that was chosen, what should be served. Today the answer is nothing at all, on the project's own corpus, for three of five built-in intents. The scoring engine is not at fault and neither is the enforcement point. What is missing is a selection rule over the product of two axes that are currently chosen independently: which projection tier to render, and how many rows to render at that tier.\n\nThe algorithm. Let B be the resolved token budget, E the envelope cost, C the ranked candidate set with a relevance score per candidate, and P an ordered projection lattice from richest tier to leanest, each tier carrying a measured cost per row. Descend the lattice. For each tier t, the feasible row count is the floor of the remaining budget after the envelope divided by the measured cost per row at t. Score that pair by the sum of marginal information over the top feasible rows, where marginal information is the candidate relevance score discounted by redundancy against the rows already selected, reusing the diversity term the packer already computes rather than introducing a second notion of redundancy. Select the tier and row count that maximise the score. The descent is over at most the size of the lattice, so the cost is linear in tiers and does not depend on corpus size.\n\nThree properties make it adoptable rather than merely plausible.\n\nCost calibration is observed, not assumed. Cost per row per tier is measured from the serialised output of recent serves, which the usage ledger already records, so no new instrumentation channel is introduced and the estimator converges on the encoding actually emitted rather than on a bytes-per-token constant. The estimator that currently under-reports by measuring a node object while the renderer emits a full payload is the negative example: the calibration input must be the emitted bytes.\n\nA feasibility floor replaces omission. When even the leanest tier admits fewer than one row, the algorithm serves exactly one row at the leanest tier and declares the overrun, rather than serving nothing. Serving nothing is never information-maximising: it delivers zero facts at a nonzero envelope cost, and the documented recovery from it costs more tokens than the payload it refused. An intent that promises rows must return rows or say plainly that the request is infeasible at any tier.\n\nThe degenerate case must be a byte-identical no-op. When the richest tier at full row count already fits the budget, the algorithm must select exactly that, so a small project sees no behavioural change and adoption cannot regress the common case. This is the falsifier: run it against a two-item workspace and diff the bytes. If the output differs there, the descent is doing something other than what it claims.\n\nInteraction with the envelope. The descent divides by the budget remaining after the envelope, so a per-page envelope re-emitted on every page directly reduces the feasible row count. Amortising chain-invariant metadata across a cursor chain is therefore not a separate optimisation but an input to this algorithm: on the measured case, eight invariant metadata blocks against a two-row page is what turns a bounded read into one that costs 13.8 times the unbounded read for the same set.\n\nWhat this entry is not. It is not the relevance scoring engine, which supplies the ordering it consumes. It is not the diversity term, which it reuses. It is not the budget contract, which declares B. It is not the ordering-slack urgency or the outcome-distance prior, which change the scores rather than the selection over projections. And it is not a compaction strategy: it never drops fields from a row that it has decided to serve, it decides which tier to serve before it serves anything."
  - created_at: "2026-07-31T06:12:41.171Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, by slug: co-mutation-cohort-inference\n\nRecorded 2026-07-31. Extends the catalog; no prior entry is renumbered or restated. Owner recorded on the algorithm portfolio registry.\n\nThe corpus shape this exists for. Measured on this tracker today: every one of 2,226 nodes reaches an outcome milestone through a typed non-related path within five hops, and 1,839 of 2,096 ordered nodes — 87.7 percent — have no predecessor at all. The graph is reachability-saturated and order-starved. Two consequences follow for any assembler that ranks candidates. Graph distance to an outcome is nearly uninformative, because almost everything is one to three hops away. Ordering position is unavailable, because almost nothing has one. The two structural signals the engine would naturally reach for are both degenerate on the shape this corpus actually has, and there is good reason to expect that shape to be typical rather than exceptional: ordering edges must be authored, and authoring them is exactly the work that gets skipped under delivery pressure.\n\nThe observation the entry exploits. Ordering was never authored, but it was recorded. Every mutation appends a history entry carrying a timestamp, an author identity, and — since harness detection shipped — an agent instance identifier. Work that was actually done together leaves a trace of having been done together, in a stream that is append-only, hash-chained, and can never be back-written. That trace is available for the entire corpus including terminal items, and reading it requires no write to any item, which matters because writes to terminal items currently re-bucket the changelog.\n\nThe algorithm. Treat the union of history streams as a sequence of mutation events, each a triple of item id, timestamp, and actor key, where the actor key is the agent instance when present and falls back to the author identity. Partition the event sequence per actor into sessions by a gap threshold: consecutive events by one actor separated by less than the threshold belong to one session. Each session yields a cohort, the set of distinct item ids it touched. Cohort membership is the raw signal.\n\nScore it as a similarity rather than an edge. For a pair of items, define co-mutation affinity as the sum over shared cohorts of an inverse-size weight — one over the logarithm of cohort size, so a session that touched four items is strong evidence of relatedness and a bulk pass that touched nine hundred is nearly none — multiplied by a recency decay on the session timestamp. This is the same intuition that makes inverse document frequency work and it is essential here, because this project's history contains large mechanical backfills whose cohorts would otherwise dominate every score.\n\nFold it into assembly as a query-conditioned term. Given a seed set — the claimed item, the item named in the request, or the items already selected by the packer — the co-mutation term for a candidate is its affinity summed against the seed set. Add it to the existing relevance score alongside metadata, graph position, recency and usage affinity. Attach the strongest contributing session to the candidate as its explanation, so a candidate never enters the context window on this signal without being able to say which recorded work session links it to the seed.\n\nWhy it is a substitute for the missing ordering rather than a duplicate of the existing graph term. The graph term answers what is structurally connected to this. The co-mutation term answers what was actually being worked on when this was being worked on, which is a different question and the one an agent resuming a task is really asking. On a corpus with a rich authored ordering the two largely agree, and the term contributes little beyond confirmation. On a corpus with no authored ordering — the common case, and this corpus — it is the only available evidence of temporal association, and it is derived from the one channel that cannot be forged.\n\nCost and scale, which is where most retrieval signals fail. Cohorts are computed by a single ordered pass over the event stream, so construction is linear in events and requires no random access. The output is a sparse item-to-cohort posting list, which is the same data structure the search index already maintains, so scoring a candidate against a seed set is a posting-list intersection bounded by the number of cohorts the seed touched rather than by corpus size. Cohorts older than the retention horizon are folded into a decayed summary and their per-item detail dropped, so the structure is bounded in the number of live cohorts rather than in the number of historical events — a decade-long corpus costs what a month-long corpus costs, at lower resolution for the distant past. The construction pass is incremental: new events extend the newest cohorts and never revisit old ones.\n\nRequired degeneracies, which are the acceptance conditions and also the falsifier.\n\nOn a workspace of a few items and a few minutes, every event falls in one session, so every cohort is the whole workspace, so the inverse-size weight drives every affinity to approximately zero and the term contributes nothing. The small end must pay no ranking distortion and no measurable latency for machinery it cannot use.\n\nOn a single-author corpus the actor partition is trivial and the signal reduces to pure temporal adjacency, which is weaker but still correct. It must not be silently stronger — a single actor is not evidence of coordination.\n\nOn a corpus with a fully authored ordering, the term must not override it. Ordering edges are asserted facts and co-mutation is inferred association; where they disagree, the assertion wins and the disagreement is surfaced rather than resolved, which is the composition rule the cross-channel-disagreement entry already establishes.\n\nUnder a token budget the explanations degrade before the candidates, consistent with every other entry in this catalog.\n\nBulk mechanical passes must be visible as such. A session that touched a thousand items in ninety seconds is a migration, not collaboration. The inverse-size weight handles this continuously, but the acceptance test should assert it explicitly: a synthetic backfill cohort must not raise any pair's affinity above the floor.\n\nThe falsifier. Run the assembler with the term enabled and disabled against the graded judgment corpus. If the term changes no ranking on a corpus with authored ordering, it is redundant and belongs behind a shape condition rather than in the default path. If it changes rankings on the small-workspace fixture at all, the degeneracy is broken and the entry is not adoptable as specified.\n\nWhat this entry is not. It is not the usage-affinity signal, which learns from what was served and acted on; this learns from what was written, which exists for the entire historical corpus rather than only for the period since serving was instrumented. It is not the trajectory projection feature, which produces an ordered observation-action-outcome artifact for training and offline evaluation; this consumes the same stream to produce a retrieval signal, and the two should share one pass over history rather than each making their own. It is not the ordering-evidence tranche, which recovers asserted prerequisite edges from item text and writes them; this asserts nothing and writes nothing. And it is explicitly not a replacement for authoring ordering — it is what makes the engine useful during the years in which ordering remains mostly unauthored, which on the measured evidence is the normal state of a real project."
  - created_at: "2026-07-31T14:19:33.829Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, by slug: state-erased-signal-recovery\n\nRecorded 2026-07-31. Extends the catalog; no prior entry is renumbered or restated. Owner recorded on the algorithm portfolio registry. Distinct from co-mutation-cohort-inference recorded earlier today: that entry infers ordering that was never authored, from the timing of co-mutation; this entry recovers ordering that WAS authored and that the lifecycle deleted. The two are complementary and their precision differs by construction - an inference versus a recorded claim.\n\nThe corpus shape this exists for. Every assembler in this catalog scores candidates against current item state. Measured on this tracker today, current item state is a lossy projection of the record, and it is lossy in exactly the dimension the assemblers most need. clearClosedBlockerSignals in the close path removes every blocked_by dependency row when an item closes, unconditionally. The consequence in the corpus: of 1,900 closed items exactly ONE retains a recorded predecessor, while the reverse spelling blocks, which no lifecycle path touches, survives on 189. The immutable stream still holds all of it - the close history entry lists dependencies among its changed_fields, and a point-in-time read reconstructs the deleted row - so the ordering facts exist, are hash-chained, and are simply not in the projection anyone queries.\n\nThe general principle, stated once so it outlives this particular defect. Item state is the current value of a fold over the stream, and the fold is not information-preserving: close deletes ordering edges, redaction marks values, compaction prunes checkpointed prefixes, and any future lifecycle rule may delete more. Therefore any relevance signal that the lifecycle can erase must be computed from the stream and not from the fold. This is not a workaround for one bug. It is a statement about which of the two representations is authoritative for scoring, and the answer is the one that is append-only.\n\nThe algorithm. Let S be an item's history stream, an ordered sequence of entries each carrying a timestamp, an author identity, an operation, a JSON patch, and before/after hashes. Define the edge-lifetime relation: for each patch operation that adds a dependency row, emit (source, target, kind, added_at, added_by); for each patch operation that removes one, emit the matching removal with removed_at and the operation that removed it. Folding these pairs yields, for every edge the workspace has ever held, an interval of validity rather than a boolean of presence. An edge deleted by a close has a closed interval whose right endpoint is the close instant; an edge still present has a half-open interval.\n\nScoring reads intervals, not presence. A candidate's ordering position is computed over the union of currently-present edges and edges whose interval ended in a lifecycle transition rather than in an explicit authored removal - the distinction is available because the removing operation is recorded, so a close-time clear is separable from a user calling update to retract a claim they no longer believe. Retracted edges are excluded; lifecycle-erased edges are included and carry a provenance marker so a consumer can see that the ordering came from the stream rather than from state. Relevance for a candidate then uses ordering depth over this recovered digraph, which on this corpus moves the input from a frontier of 1,845 of 2,104 nodes to a digraph whose predecessor coverage is bounded below by the number of ordering edges ever authored rather than by the number that survived.\n\nComplexity and why it is affordable at scale. The fold is linear in stream length and touches only entries whose patch mentions dependencies, which is a small fraction: on this tracker 44,408 entries across 2,244 streams, of which dependency-bearing entries are a low-thousands subset. Critically the fold is monotonic in stream length, because the stream is append-only, so the result is cacheable keyed by (stream id, entry count, tail hash) and an incremental update reads only entries appended since the cached point. This is the same content-addressed discipline the attested-reads work uses, and it composes with it: an assembly that recovered ordering from the stream can attest which entry ranges it read.\n\nSmall-project and large-project behaviour. On a workspace of a few items spanning minutes, the fold is a handful of entries and the recovered digraph equals the authored one, because nothing has closed yet - the algorithm degenerates gracefully to reading current state and costs nothing. On a workspace of millions of items spanning decades, the recovery is per-item and embarrassingly parallel, the cache key makes re-reads free, and the only global step is the union, which is a streaming merge. Compaction is the one interaction that needs care: a checkpointed and pruned prefix loses the patch detail this fold depends on, so the compaction path must either retain dependency-affecting patches or record the derived edge intervals into the checkpoint before pruning. That is a real constraint on the compaction design and should be stated there rather than discovered later.\n\nThe falsifiable claim, and how to test it without trusting this note. Recover intervals for the whole corpus and count predecessor coverage over the recovered digraph. If the deletion hypothesis is right, coverage rises by roughly the number of blocked_by rows ever added minus those explicitly retracted, and the recovered predecessors for closed items should reconcile against the 189 surviving blocks rows on the other endpoint - the same real prerequisites recorded from the opposite direction should appear in both populations. If recovered coverage barely moves, the deletion is not the dominant cause and the ordering deficit is genuinely an authoring gap after all, which would falsify both this entry and the finding it rests on. That reconciliation is the test worth running first, because it is cheap and it discriminates between the two competing explanations.\n\nWhat this entry does not claim. It does not propose changing the close semantics; that is a product decision tracked separately. It also does not assert that a lifecycle-erased edge is still a live constraint - it is explicitly historical, and a scheduler must not treat a recovered edge as an unmet prerequisite. The recovery is for understanding what happened, which is what a context assembler needs, and not for deciding what is actionable, which is what readiness needs. Conflating those two is the mistake that made blocked_by carry both meanings in the first place."
  - created_at: "2026-08-01T09:51:18.539Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, by slug: occupancy-interval-contention-inference\n\nRecorded 2026-08-01. Extends the catalog; no prior entry is renumbered or restated. Owner recorded on the algorithm portfolio registry (pm-g8t7b9). Filed alongside pm-1wujzi, which owns the missing read surface this entry consumes.\n\nThe gap this closes, stated against an entry that already exists. Entry (24), fleet-joint context diversification, conditions each agent's selection on what its peers already hold, and justifies the assumption in one sentence: \"the claim ledger says who holds what ... readable without new distributed machinery.\" That sentence is half true and the false half is load-bearing. The ledger does say who holds what. Reading it is a fold over one item's entire history stream, performed by resolveClaimStateContext in the single-item get path and reachable from nowhere else, so approximating a peer set of P agents costs P stream folds, and asking the workspace-level question — which of N items is held — costs N. On this tracker that is 2,258 folds over 42,771 entries for one query. Entry (24) is correct as an objective and unimplementable as specified until occupancy is served from maintained state.\n\nBut the more interesting observation is that the fold discards more than it costs. It keeps the last claim and the last release. The stream holds every claim and every release, each with a timestamp and an author identity, which means occupancy is not a boolean at now — it is an interval-valued relation over the whole history, and the intervals are the signal.\n\nThe algorithm. Let S be the union of history streams. Project the subsequence of entries whose op is claim or release, each carrying (item, ts, actor), where actor is the agent instance when present and falls back to author identity. Fold per item into a sequence of occupancy intervals: a claim opens an interval, a matching release closes it, a claim while an interval is open closes the previous one as superseded, and an interval still open at head is live. Annotate each interval with whether any content-mutating op for that item falls strictly inside it — the ops are already distinguishable in the stream: update, comment_add, files_add, tests_add, docs_add, note_add, learning_add, append, close.\n\nReconstructed on this corpus today, which is what makes the entry adoptable rather than speculative: 2,109 productive intervals with mutation during hold, 25 intervals with a clean claim/release and no mutation between them, and 295 intervals superseded by a later claim without an intervening release. Zero live intervals at the moment of measurement, so the current-occupancy term is exercised only under load, while the historical terms are available immediately over the whole corpus.\n\nThree signals fall out, and they are different questions that a single boolean cannot answer.\n\nLive avoidance. The set of items with an open interval, with holder identity. This is entry (24)'s peer input, and once occupancy is maintained state rather than a per-item fold, it is a set membership test rather than a scan.\n\nContention prior. Occupancy density per item and, by projection through the typed graph, per neighbourhood: how often a region of the project has been held by more than one actor within a window. A region with a high contention prior is expensive to allocate into regardless of who holds it right now, which is information an allocator (pm-3oqjnc) needs before it has any live signal at all — on a cold fleet, live occupancy is empty and the prior is the only evidence available.\n\nAbandonment. An interval with no mutation between claim and release is a recorded attempt that produced nothing. Twenty-five of them on this corpus is a small number and that is exactly why it is useful: abandonment is rare enough that each instance is a strong signal about the item rather than noise about the process. Ranked into an assembly, an abandoned attempt is high-value context for the next agent to approach the same work — someone tried this and stopped, and the stream says who and when, so the surrounding cohort of what they touched instead is recoverable through co-mutation-cohort-inference.\n\nHand-off continuity, which is the composition rather than a fourth signal. Two adjacent intervals on one item held by different actors form an implicit hand-off. For an agent resuming work, the previous holder's session cohort is a better seed set than the item's own graph neighbourhood, because it is what was actually in play when the work was last touched.\n\nCost and scale. The projection is a single ordered pass over the claim/release subsequence, which is a small fraction of entries — 5,026 of 42,771 here, under twelve percent. The fold is monotonic in stream length because the stream is append-only, so the result is cacheable keyed by (stream id, entry count, tail hash) with incremental extension, the same content-addressed discipline state-erased-signal-recovery and the attested-reads work already use, and the two should share one pass over history rather than each making their own. Live occupancy is a bounded set — at most one entry per concurrently held item — so serving it is O(held) rather than O(corpus), which is the property that makes the fleet question affordable at a million items. Historical density folds into a decayed summary past the retention horizon, so a decade of history costs what a month costs at lower resolution for the distant past.\n\nRequired degeneracies, which are the acceptance conditions and the falsifier.\n\nOn a single-agent workspace every interval has the same actor, so the contention prior is identically zero and live avoidance never fires. The small end must pay nothing: no ranking distortion, no measurable latency, and the assembly must be byte-identical to today.\n\nOn a workspace where claiming is not used at all — which is permitted, since claim is advisory — every term is identically zero and the engine behaves exactly as it does now. The algorithm must never manufacture occupancy from assignee, which is a mutable metadata field that survives release and is set on 288 items here without implying that any of them is held.\n\nAbandonment must not be inferred from a short interval. A claim released in ninety seconds after a one-line correction is productive; a claim held for a day with no mutation is abandoned. The discriminator is mutation-during-hold, never duration, because duration measures the agent and mutation measures the work.\n\nContention must decay. An item contended six months ago and quiet since is not contended. Without decay the prior monotonically accumulates and eventually ranks the oldest items highest, which is the failure mode every recency-free signal in this catalog has been designed against.\n\nThe falsifier. Compute the contention prior over the corpus and correlate it against rework — items whose history shows a close followed by a reopening or a superseding item. If contention density carries no signal about rework, the prior is decorative and belongs behind a shape condition rather than in the default path. That test is cheap, runs entirely offline against recorded history, and needs no change to any write path to perform.\n\nWhat this entry is not. It is not entry (24), which decides what each agent sees given a peer set; this supplies and extends the peer set. It is not pm-3oqjnc, which decides which agent works on which item; this supplies a prior it consumes. It is not co-mutation-cohort-inference, which infers association from mutation timing across items; this reads a declared, explicitly recorded relation between an actor and one item, and the two compose — cohorts explain what a holder was doing, intervals explain when they held it. It is not the usage-affinity signal, which learns from what was served. And it asserts nothing about whether claiming ought to be mandatory; it reads what was recorded and stays silent about what should have been."
  - created_at: "2026-08-01T13:07:13.153Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry, by slug: refusal-conditioned-retrieval-repair\n\nRecorded 2026-08-01. Extends the catalog; no prior entry is restarted and none is renumbered. Distinct from refuted-and-abandoned-evidence-retrieval, which scores tracker evidence that was later refuted; this entry scores nothing in the tracker at all. Its input is the agent's own failed invocation.\n\nGap this closes. Every relevance signal in the engine today is derived from recorded state: item fields, graph position, history, usage affinity, occupancy. None of them observe the single richest predictor of what an agent needs next, which is what it just tried and was refused. A refusal carries the command, the flag, the rejected value, the legal domain when the failure site knows it, and the nearest match. Today that payload is emitted once to stderr and discarded. The next assembly is computed as if the refusal never happened, so an agent that guessed a projection field wrong receives exactly the same context it received before guessing, and its cheapest recovery is to widen the read.\n\nMechanism. Maintain a bounded per-session refusal trace, keyed by the same session identity the usage ledger already carries. Each entry holds the command path, the offending flag or positional, the rejected value, the declared domain if one was rendered, and the outcome of the following invocation. Three uses, in increasing ambition.\n\nFirst, domain injection. When the next assembly serves the same command, the declared domain of any flag refused in this session is included verbatim and unbudgeted, because it is small, bounded by the contract table, and provably the thing the agent lacked. This is a certainty, not an estimate, so it enters ahead of any scored candidate.\n\nSecond, repair-distance ranking. Score candidates by edit distance from the rejected value to legal values, and promote the candidate set that would have satisfied the attempt. A rejected --fields value that is close to a real projectable field implies the agent has a field-level information need, so field-bearing candidates outrank narrative ones for that turn.\n\nThird, refusal-rate feedback into the surface itself. Aggregate refusals across the fleet by command and flag. A flag whose refusal rate stays high after its domain is rendered is a naming defect, not a discovery defect, and that is a measurable input to the consolidation programme rather than a guess.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, which the catalog requires of every entry. On a session with zero refusals the term is identically zero and the assembly is byte-identical to today. On a corpus of one item it is identically zero. It must never promote a candidate purely because a refusal occurred; the refusal conditions the ranking, it does not manufacture relevance. The trace is session-scoped and bounded, so it cannot grow into a second usage ledger, and it must be readable without a write, so it cannot repeat the read-rewrites-itself defect the usage ledger has.\n\nPrerequisite. The declared domain has to actually be present in the refusal before any of this can key on it. That is the discovery defect filed today, so this entry is downstream of it and is not implementable first."
  - created_at: "2026-08-02T15:04:50.169Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (23) — citation-stable evidence addressing.\n\nExtends the catalog; does not restart it. Every prior entry (1)-(22) assumes that once the engine selects an evidence unit it can name it. Horizon Y (note on pm-doxj, 2026-08-02) shows the naming layer is itself unstable: history-compact rebases version numbers so a citation emitted today resolves to different content after the next compaction (pm-m0yjtg), and nothing in the record lets a reader detect it.\n\nMechanism. The assembly step already returns candidates with a provenance trail. Extend the candidate contract so every emitted reference carries an addressing tuple {scheme, value, stability_class, generation}, and make the packer prefer the most stable scheme that fits the budget:\n- content hash (immutable, ~64 chars) when the citation must survive arbitrarily long,\n- timestamp (stable under compaction, refuses correctly when pruned) as the default,\n- version number (rebasable) only inside a single response, never in durable text.\nCost model: the scheme choice is a per-citation token decision, so it belongs in the same budget-feasible descent as slug budget-feasible-projection-descent rather than as a separate pass - a hash costs roughly 4x a version number, and the packer should spend it only where the citation outlives the response.\n\nResolution behaviour. A reader presented with a stale reference must get one of exactly two outcomes: the same content, or a declared refusal naming the generation that superseded it. Silently resolving to different content is the failure this entry exists to eliminate, and it is the single acceptance test.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, per the catalog's standing requirement that every entry be identically inert on corpora that cannot exhibit the phenomenon:\n- On a workspace where no stream has ever been compacted, redacted, repaired, or merged, every scheme has one generation, so scheme selection is a constant and the output is byte-identical to today.\n- On a corpus below the compaction threshold the term never activates, so the small-project end (pm-z436) pays nothing.\n- Generation count is monotone and cheap to read from the baseline entries, so the signal costs one integer per stream, not a scan.\n\nServes. pm-qd6gvf (trajectory projection - an episode that cites unstable names is not replayable, which is the whole point of the artifact), pm-e1vkee (verdict contract - reproducibility is its declared boundary), pm-3z0k (detached attestation - a bundle whose references rebase is not a proof), pm-e5yupa (multi-decade readability), pm-h6wmtc (retention, which is the operation that forces the rebase). Owner map goes on pm-g8t7b9 with entries (19)-(22)."
  - created_at: "2026-08-02T21:14:06.605Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (24) — convergence-confidence weighting of cited evidence.\n\nExtends the catalog; does not restart it, and no prior entry is renumbered. Entries (1)-(23) all assume the workspace the engine reads is THE workspace: a cited item is either recorded or it is not, and once entry (23) fixed the naming layer, a citation is stable. Horizon Z (note on pm-doxj, 2026-08-02) removes that assumption. Under a fleet on branches, the record an assembly cites may be one side of a combination that another clone resolved the other way — the value the engine cited was destroyed in a peer workspace and nothing in the local record says so.\n\nSignal: for each candidate evidence unit, compute a convergence confidence from three inputs already present in recorded state — (a) the combination law of the field the citation rests on, since a value in a union collection is safe against direction while a scalar is not; (b) whether the holder carries an unreconciled merge receipt, which marks a field whose value was chosen rather than recorded; (c) the number of merge boundaries between the cited version and the reader's head, read from the history stream's reanchor points. Rank evidence that is invariant under combination above evidence that merely happens to have survived, and when a low-confidence unit must be cited, say so in the assembly rather than presenting it flat.\n\nInteraction with entry (8) recency and entry (23) citation stability: recency answers when, (23) answers whether the name still points at the same thing, and (24) answers whether the thing would still be there had the branches merged the other way. All three are cheap reads over data the engine already loads.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, mandatory as in (23): on a workspace with one branch and no merge receipts every unit scores identically, the signal must contribute nothing to the ranking and must cost no additional read; on a workspace whose entire corpus sits in union collections the signal is constant and must be skipped rather than computed. The measure is that a single-agent minutes-old project pays exactly zero for it.\n\nEvidence: pm-dlx7v7 (direction decides the survivor), pm-inn5y5 (a list-shaped field in the scalar class loses concurrent additions), pm-qx95lz (two items fused under one id with no finding)."
  - created_at: "2026-08-03T06:28:46.897Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (25) — session-marginal relevance ranking.\n\nExtends the catalog; does not restart it. Every prior entry stands unchanged and none is restated. Entries 1 through 24 all score a candidate against the QUERY. Entry 25 is the first that scores it against the CALLER: relevance is redefined as marginal information with respect to what this session has already been served.\n\nProblem it addresses. Every ranking mechanism in the catalog computes a score for an item given a request, and each is correct in isolation. Because they are correct in isolation, they agree, and their agreement is expensive: measured on 2026-08-03 against the real 2,285-item tracker, the prescribed orientation sequence emits 110 item mentions to deliver 76 distinct items, with the highest-ranked items serialized in full up to three times inside one cold start. Absolute relevance has no way to express that the second delivery of a fact is worth much less than the first, because the scoring function cannot see the first.\n\nMechanism. The caller carries a held set: the identities, and optionally the field-level depth, of facts already delivered within a declared request group. Scoring becomes two-term. The first term is the existing multi-signal relevance, unchanged, so every prior catalog entry survives intact as the base measure. The second term is a delivery discount applied per candidate as a function of what the held set already contains about that candidate - full suppression when the same projection depth was already served, partial discount when a shallower projection was served and the candidate now qualifies for a deeper one. Selection then maximizes total marginal information under the group budget rather than total relevance under a per-call budget, which is the standard submodular-coverage shape: the greedy order is provably within a constant factor of optimal, and the discount makes the objective diminishing-returns by construction rather than by tuning.\n\nRepresentation constraint, load-bearing. The held set must travel with the request, not be stored per workspace. Storing it makes the mechanism cost something at three items, breaks under concurrent sessions from one agent, and creates a durable per-caller structure that the multi-decade retention policy would then have to reason about. Travelling makes it free at small scale, stateless across branches, and identical in shape to the across-agent case, where the held set is a shared attested assembly rather than one session's history. The within-session and across-fleet mechanisms must therefore be one mechanism with one representation.\n\nSuppression must be visible, never silent. A candidate withheld because it was already delivered is emitted as a resolvable reference carrying its identity and the reason, so the answer remains self-contained and an agent that lost its earlier context can recover. Silent suppression would reintroduce, at the sequence level, exactly the omission-without-receipt failure the per-call contract already forbids.\n\nCalibration risk to state plainly. The discount curve is a behavioural constant, and this project has an open finding that its constants were fitted to one corpus without recording which are principled. The discount must be derived rather than fitted: full suppression at equal projection depth is a logical identity, not a tuned parameter, and any intermediate value must be justified by a measured information difference, not by what makes the current corpus look good.\n\nEvaluation. The existing judgment corpus and evaluation harness score answer quality for a single call and remain the correctness floor: marginal ranking must not reduce the quality of any first answer. The new measure is sequence-level - tokens per completed task across a whole prescribed protocol - which the golden-transcript replay work already proposes as a CI-visible regression gate. Both must move in the right direction for the entry to be adopted; a cheaper sequence that answers the first question worse is a regression.\n\nRelation to the other entries. Composes with, and does not replace, the recency, graph-proximity, usage-affinity and budget-feasible-descent entries: it multiplies their output rather than competing with it. Distinct from the delta-read projection, which is the degenerate case where the held set is exactly everything before a cursor and the discount is total. Owned by pm-4k6b for the scoring change, pm-hid9g1 for the accounting unit it requires, and horizon AA on pm-doxj for the argument that the sequence deserves a contract at all."
  - created_at: "2026-08-03T20:40:23.212Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (26) — recurrence-conditioned retrieval priors.\n\nExtends the catalog. Entries 1 through 25 stand unchanged and none is restated. Entries 1 through 24 score a candidate against the query. Entry 25 scores it against what this session has already been served. Entry 26 scores it against how often the situation it describes has come back.\n\nProblem it addresses. The engine treats every recorded fact as a sample from a stationary past. A closed item that was resolved once and never returned, and a closed item belonging to a family that has returned thirty times, are indistinguishable inputs: both are terminal, both decay by recency, both carry the same edge kinds. Yet their future value to a caller is not remotely the same. The first is history. The second is a prediction. Measured on this corpus, one defect family accounts for roughly thirty closed items across eight months and returns on half of all days, and nothing in the ranking distinguishes any of its members from a one-time fix closed the same week.\n\nThe mechanism. Define a family as a set of items joined by an explicit recurrence identity. For each family, derive from the immutable record alone an occurrence count, an inter-arrival distribution over the timestamps of its members, a mean repair interval, and a trend on those inter-arrivals. From these derive a hazard: the probability that this family produces a new occurrence within the caller's horizon. The retrieval prior for any member is then raised by the family hazard, and raised further when the current request touches an artifact, file, error code or platform that the family's evidence names.\n\nThree properties make this different from recency and from frequency. It is forward-looking rather than backward: a family whose last occurrence is old but whose period is long scores high, while a burst that has demonstrably ended decays. It is defined on the family and inherited by members, so a single old member of a live family is retrieved on the strength of its siblings, which is exactly the retrieval a caller about to repeat the defect needs. And it is asymmetric with respect to resolution quality: a family whose members were each closed with strong evidence and still returned is more informative than one closed weakly, because the recorded fixes are known not to have been sufficient.\n\nInteraction with existing entries. It composes with the ordering-slack urgency term by supplying a second source of urgency that is not derived from the dependency graph. It composes with trust-weighted assembly by weighting a family's evidence up rather than down, which is the opposite direction and must not be folded into the same coefficient. It is strictly distinct from entry 22, lineage-aware relevance continuity: continuity follows mass through copy, split and supersede, where one thing becomes another. Recurrence follows identity through independent occurrences, where a thing happens again. It is distinct from entry 20, temporal non-stationarity mining, which detects eras in the corpus as a whole rather than periodicity within one family.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, mandatory. On a corpus where no family has more than one member, every hazard is undefined and the term must contribute identically zero, not a small default. On a young corpus where inter-arrival estimates rest on fewer members than a declared minimum, the term must be suppressed rather than estimated from one interval, and the suppression must be visible in the explain output. On a corpus with no recurrence identities recorded at all, the entry must be a byte-identical no-op against the engine without it. Where hierarchical shrinkage from entry 21 is available, family hazards are shrunk toward the global recurrence base rate rather than trusted raw.\n\nBlocking prerequisite. The entry is not implementable until a recurrence identity exists, which is now owned separately under the graph epic. Until then a family cannot be enumerated except by parsing titles, and a ranking signal derived from text parsing is not admissible as a scoring input."
  - created_at: "2026-08-04T05:54:30.594Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (27) — temporally anchored graph proximity.\n\nExtends the catalog. Entries 1 through 26 stand unchanged and none is restated. Entries 1 through 24 score a candidate against the query. Entry 25 scores it against what this session has already been served. Entry 26 scores it against how often its defect family has recurred. Entry 27 scores it against the graph as it stood at the moment the query is about, which is not the graph that exists now.\n\nThe signal. Every relationship edge in this tracker carries a creation timestamp, and coverage is exactly 100 percent over all 9,152 authored rows, verified this session by direct census of the item files rather than through a graph command. Nothing in the scoring pipeline reads that timestamp. Every graph-derived feature the engine can currently compute, degree, centrality, path distance, community membership, dominance, slack, is computed against the HEAD snapshot only.\n\nWhy HEAD is the wrong graph for a large class of queries. On this corpus 7,863 of 9,152 edges, 86 percent, were authored during 2026-07, in a backfill campaign. An item closed in April therefore looks far better connected today than it was on the day the work happened. Any relevance score that uses graph position as a proxy for importance is, for every historical query, reading a property the item acquired after the fact. The engine confidently ranks on a signal that did not exist when the thing it ranks was true. This is not noise that averages out; it is a monotone distortion that grows with the distance between the query anchor and now.\n\nThe scoring rule. Give every query a temporal anchor. For forward-looking intents, next work, current blockers, what to claim, the anchor is now and entry 27 degenerates to the present behaviour, which is correct. For retrospective intents, why did this break, what shipped in that release, what did this incident touch, what was blocking us then, the anchor is the timestamp the query is about, taken from a named release, an incident window, an explicit selector, or the anchor of the item under examination.\n\nThen weight each edge by the distance between its assertion time and the anchor rather than treating all edges as equally present. Two regimes, one function. Edges asserted after the anchor are evidence about the future of the anchor and are discounted toward zero for reconstruction intents, because including them is exactly the leakage described above. Edges asserted before the anchor are discounted by age with a half-life, so that a relationship asserted the week before the anchor outweighs one asserted a year earlier, which encodes the ordinary fact that recent structure is more explanatory of a moment than ancient structure.\n\nThe same function serves the forward case with the anchor set to now, where it does useful work rather than degenerating: it damps the influence of very old edges on present recommendations, which is the graph-side analogue of the recency signal entries 1 through 24 already apply to item fields. One implementation, both regimes, selected by the anchor alone.\n\nInteraction with the existing catalog. Entry 26 conditions on recurrence, and recurrence is measured over history, so entry 26 is only sound when the edges it counts are the edges that existed at each occurrence. Entry 27 is therefore a precondition for 26 being correct rather than merely a companion to it. Against entry 25, session novelty, the two are orthogonal and compose by multiplication. Against the ordering signals, an as-of blocker set is what makes the question of how long a chain actually held answerable at all, since at HEAD every resolved chain is simply absent.\n\nCost and bounding. The naive implementation replays history per query and is not viable at the scales this engine targets. The tractable form is a time-indexed projection of the durable graph index keyed by assertion time, so that an as-of edge set is a range scan rather than a reconstruction, with replay reserved for anchors older than the retained index horizon and for verification. The budget contract is unchanged: an as-of scoring pass inherits the same row and token bounds as its HEAD equivalent, and the cost receipt separates index cost from replay cost so a caller can see which regime it paid for. Entry 27 is unimplementable until the projection it needs exists, which is the feature filed this session as pm-3yzyfe, and that item is the correct dependency to carry.\n\nFalsifiable claim, so this entry can be retired if wrong. On this corpus, ranking the same retrospective query at HEAD and at its anchor must produce materially different orderings. If the two orderings agree within noise, the backfill did not distort historical position and entry 27 buys nothing but cost. The measurement is cheap and should be taken before the scoring change is built, not after."
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T05:23:58.135Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (28) — act-conditioned loss asymmetry: rank by expected regret under the caller's declared act, not by expected relevance.\n\nExtends the catalog. Entries 1 through 27 stand unchanged and none is restated. Entries 1 through 24 score a candidate against the query. Entry 25 scores it against what this session has already been served. Entry 26 scores it against how often its defect family has recurred. Entry 27 scores it against the graph as it stood at the moment the query is about. Entry 28 is the first that scores it against what the caller is about to do, and it is the first whose objective is a loss rather than a value.\n\nProblem it addresses. Every entry so far estimates how likely a candidate is to be wanted, and then serves the highest estimates until the budget binds. That is correct when the cost of a mistake is symmetric, and the cost of a mistake is almost never symmetric. An agent about to mutate an item pays an unbounded price for one omitted blocking constraint and a negligible price for one surplus row: the omission produces a refusal, a wedged claim, a cycle, or a merge that discards a field, and every one of those costs another turn and another set of reads. An agent about to summarise for a human pays the reverse: a surplus row is the entire cost, and a missing edge case changes nothing. Ranking by relevance serves both callers the same page, so it is over-inclusive for one and dangerously under-inclusive for the other, and the budget machinery makes this worse rather than better, because when a ceiling binds it drops the tail uniformly with no notion of which tail item was load-bearing.\n\nMechanism. The caller's act is already declared: the context-intent vocabulary names it, and a mutation surface knows its own verb. Condition the ranking on it. For each candidate define two losses rather than one score. The omission loss is the expected additional cost the caller incurs if the candidate is withheld and turns out to have mattered. The inclusion loss is the delivery cost of the candidate itself, which the token accounting already measures in emitted bytes. Rank by the difference, which is expected regret, and let the budget bind on regret rather than on relevance. The existing multi-signal relevance from entries 1 through 24 becomes the probability term inside the omission loss, so every prior entry survives intact as the estimator it always was; entry 25's delivery discount multiplies the inclusion loss, since a fact the caller already holds costs nothing to withhold; entry 26's family hazard raises the omission loss for recurring families; entry 27 supplies the graph position as of the query time rather than as of now.\n\nWhy the loss function is derivable and not a hand-tuned constant, which is what makes this entry belong to this project rather than to a paper. The immutable record already contains the ground truth. For every served read there is a subsequent trajectory in the same streams: whether the caller's next mutation was refused, whether it produced a dependency cycle, whether a claim collided, whether a merge discarded a field, whether the item was reopened. Join a served projection against what the caller did next and the omission cost stops being an assumption and becomes a measurement: the empirical additional-turn cost of having withheld a row of that kind, under that act, on this corpus. The estimator is therefore fit from the record and re-fit as the record grows, which is the only form of adaptivity this project accepts, because it is auditable — the fit is reproducible from the streams that produced it.\n\nInteraction with the budget contract, stated precisely so it cannot be read as a licence to overrun. Regret ranking never raises a declared ceiling. It changes only which rows survive the ceiling and in which order they are dropped. Under a binding budget the receipt gains one term: the act it conditioned on, spelled as the flag that would change it. A caller who declares no act gets the unconditioned ranking, and the receipt says so.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, mandatory for every catalog entry. When the act is undeclared, the term is identically zero and the ranking reduces exactly to entry 25's output. When the record contains no post-read trajectory for an act, the omission loss falls back to uniform and the difference collapses to relevance minus delivery cost, which is entry 25 again. On a corpus small enough that every candidate fits inside the budget, ranking cannot change the served set at all, so the projection is byte-identical, and that identity is the acceptance test on a two-item workspace. No degenerate case may emit a different byte than today.\n\nWhat this entry does not claim. It does not predict the caller's next action, and it must not be implemented as a predictor. It prices the consequence of being wrong about a candidate, conditioned on an act the caller stated. The distinction matters, because a predictor that guesses the act would reintroduce exactly the invisible default that ruling L forbids.\n\nRelation to the rest of the catalog and to the horizon ledger. Entry 28 is the read-side complement of horizon AC: AC observes that a published measurement does not constrain anything, and 28 is the first catalog entry whose parameters are constrained by measurements taken from the record itself. It is also the mechanism by which the recursive-self-improvement loop finally has something to optimise on the retrieval side, because regret is a number that can be shown to have moved."
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T11:23:09.664Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (29) — structure-conditioned signal weighting: the graph term earns its weight from the measured resolution of the graph, not from a fitted constant.\n\nExtends the catalog. Entries 1 through 28 stand unchanged.\n\n## The problem this entry names\n\nEvery graph-derived relevance signal in this catalog — typed distance to the caller's outcome, dominator overlap, community co-membership, blast-radius rank — assumes the typed graph has resolution: that different candidates sit at different structural distances from what the caller is doing. The assumption was never checked.\n\nMeasured 2026-08-07 over all 2,357 items, following only typed edges and excluding the two untyped kinds: 1,024 of 2,007 terminal items reach the roadmap apex and no outcome milestone at all, and the 350 active items occupy exactly four distinct hop distances, with 282 of them at one or two. For most candidate sets the engine could assemble today, typed distance is a constant. A scorer multiplying a fitted weight against a constant produces a confident number that carries no information, and — worse — the constant is not zero, so the graph term systematically displaces terms that do carry information.\n\nThis is the same class as the calibration hazard already recorded on the behavioural-constants item: every threshold in this engine was fitted against one corpus. Entry (29) is narrower and structural. It is not that the weight is wrong for other corpora. It is that the weight is wrong for *this* corpus at *this* query, and the engine has no way to notice.\n\n## Mechanism one — informativeness gating\n\nFor each assembly, after candidate generation and before scoring, compute the distribution of the structural feature over the candidate set and take its normalized Shannon entropy. Concretely, for typed distance to the caller's outcome anchor, bucket the candidates by hop count, form the empirical distribution over occupied buckets, and divide by the log of the bucket count. The result is a number in zero to one that answers a single question: does this feature separate these candidates?\n\nThe graph term's weight for that assembly is its declared prior multiplied by that number. Entropy near zero means every candidate is structurally identical, the term contributes nothing, and the budget it would have consumed is reallocated proportionally across the terms whose own informativeness is non-zero — recency, usage affinity, lifecycle salience. Entropy near one means the structure genuinely separates the candidates and the term carries its full declared prior.\n\nThree properties make this the right shape rather than a heuristic.\n\nIt is self-correcting across the corpus lifetime. When the terminal backfill waves land and hop distances spread from four buckets to ten, the same statistic raises the graph term's weight automatically. No retune, no new constant, and no migration — the tuning surface is the corpus, which is what changed.\n\nIt spans the declared scale spectrum without a mode switch. On a three-item scratch workspace every candidate is at distance zero or one, entropy collapses, and the engine degrades to recency ordering, which is the correct behaviour at that size and is currently achieved only by a separate small-workspace code path. At a million items with a deep graph, entropy is high and structure dominates. One statistic replaces the branch.\n\nIt is honest about its own inputs, which is the property the attestation work requires. The per-term informativeness values are exactly the receipt an attested assembly needs to be reproducible and checkable: they say not only what was returned but which signals were load-bearing and which were inert.\n\nThe refusal boundary matters and is stated here so it cannot be over-applied. Informativeness gating decides *weight*, never *membership*. A candidate is never excluded because a feature failed to separate it. Gating a term to zero must be visible in the receipt rather than silent, because a zeroed graph term is a diagnosis of the graph, and the diagnosis is worth more than the score.\n\n## Mechanism two — bounded exact distance at corpus scale\n\nInformativeness gating requires typed distance for every candidate on every assembly, which a per-query breadth-first search cannot supply inside the latency and token budgets at a million items. Use pruned landmark labeling over the typed subgraph.\n\nChoose landmarks as the outcome milestones plus the highest-betweenness hubs, on the order of a few dozen. Precompute, for each node, the distance to each landmark that is not already covered by a closer landmark's label — the standard two-hop cover pruning. Exact distance between any node and any landmark is then a bounded label lookup rather than a traversal, and the label set is small precisely because this graph is a shallow star, which is the one respect in which the structural defect helps.\n\nMaintenance is incremental and append-only, which matches the storage model. An added edge can only shorten distances, so it triggers a bounded relaxation from the edge's endpoints rather than a rebuild. Edge removal is the expensive direction and is rare here; it schedules a background rebuild rather than blocking a read, and until the rebuild lands the labels are conservatively stale in the safe direction, meaning distances may be overstated and the informativeness statistic understated, which biases toward under-weighting structure rather than over-trusting it.\n\nThe label set is content-addressed by the hash of the typed edge set. That makes it exactly the artifact the fleet-amortized assembly work needs: a thousand agents asking a thousand different questions against one unchanged graph share one label computation, and a single edge write invalidates precisely the labels it touches rather than the whole index.\n\n## Sequencing and dependencies\n\nThis entry cannot be validated before the graph has resolution to measure. Its natural order is: the ladder-topology disposition and the terminal backfill waves establish real depth; the relevance evaluation harness records a pre-change baseline against the degenerate graph, which is a valuable baseline precisely because it is degenerate; then gating lands and the harness measures whether assemblies improved. If the harness shows no improvement after depth appears, that is evidence the structural family of signals does not pay for itself in this domain, and the catalog should record that finding rather than defend the family.\n\n## What this entry deliberately does not propose\n\nNo learned weights. No embedding-based structural similarity. Both were considered and both fail the reproducibility requirement that attested assemblies impose: an assembly must be recomputable from recorded state alone, and a learned weight is recorded state only if the training corpus and procedure are also recorded, which is a much larger commitment than this entry is making. Entropy over an observed distribution is computed from the candidate set itself and is trivially reproducible."
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T17:23:07.639Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (30) — cost-priced signal admission: a signal enters the scoring function only when its marginal information per unit of compute clears the budget's exchange rate, and the served envelope names which signals were admitted at this corpus size.\n\nEntries (1) through (29) stand unchanged.\n\nMotivation, measured 2026-08-07. The most informative structural signal available to this engine is exact shortest-path betweenness over the relationship graph, and it is the one signal whose cost does not survive the corpus sizes this project has declared. Cold, on the live tracker at 2,365 nodes and 11,318 edges, it takes 7,023 ms; warm it takes 804. The durable index that produces the warm number is keyed by query shape and excluded from version control, so every clone, CI job and agent worktree begins cold and the seven-second path is the one automation always takes. The declared scale tiers are 10,000, 100,000 and 1,000,000 items, and exact betweenness is theta of nodes times edges. Filed as pm-431035.\n\nThe engine cannot therefore treat structural signals as free inputs to a weighted sum. Entry (29) already establishes that the graph term earns its weight from the measured resolution of the graph rather than a fitted constant. (30) is the admission gate that must sit in front of it: resolution is a benefit, and benefit alone cannot decide whether to consult a signal that costs four orders of magnitude more at the next tier.\n\nShape of the entry. Each signal carries a measured cost class as a function of corpus size and a measured marginal contribution to ranking quality, both re-derived from the record rather than declared as constants. The budget in force implies an exchange rate between compute and served relevance. A signal is admitted when its marginal contribution per unit cost clears that rate at the current corpus size, and refused otherwise. Refusal is not silent: the envelope names the admitted set, so a caller can tell a ranking computed with structural evidence from one computed without it. An approximate variant of a refused signal — sampled betweenness, bounded-depth reachability — is admitted on its own measured cost and its own measured contribution, never inheriting the exact variant's.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, mandatory as for every entry in this catalog. On a corpus where every signal clears the rate, the admitted set is the full set and the ranking is byte-identical to the unpriced ranking, so a small project sees no behaviour change and pays no receipt. On a corpus of one item every signal has zero marginal contribution and the entry must not divide by zero: the admission decision degenerates to admit-all rather than refuse-all, because refusing every signal on a trivial corpus would serve an empty ranking, which entry (12)'s feasibility floor already forbids.\n\nWhat this entry must not become. It is not a second budget system; the ceiling and the pre-call calibrated expectation remain pm-gd6vnn's, resolving into pm-5t33or. It is not a cache policy; the durable index's entry bound belongs to pm-431035. It prices consulting a signal, and nothing else."
  - created_at: "2026-08-08T08:04:42.076Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Context relevance ledger (31): the cheapest high-value context signal available today is the set of contracts whose reader and writer were last edited in different changes.\n\nRecorded 2026-08-08. Extends the ledger; entries 1 through 30 stand.\n\nWhy this belongs to relevance and not only to CI. The engine ranks what an agent should look at next. Today's incident says one class of item is systematically underranked: the item that is fine in isolation and broken only in relation to another item that changed. The release gate was correct. The exception reporter was correct. The pair was not, and no signal in the corpus expresses pairness, so nothing could have surfaced it before it halted the pipeline.\n\nThe signal, concretely. For any declared contract that one side resolves against a catalog, compute the interval between the last change to the reader and the last change to the writer, and the presence or absence of a test that exercises both in one process. A reader rebound to a stricter contract with no co-edited writer and no joint test is the highest-risk shape in the corpus, and it is derivable entirely from data already recorded: linked files per item, commit adjacency, and the generated catalog membership that both sides already reference. It needs no new instrumentation.\n\nWhy it ranks well. It is sparse, which matters under a token budget — most contracts have co-edited sides and drop out. It is temporal, so it decays correctly as parity is established. And it is explainable in one sentence, which the ranking surface requires: this reader got stricter on a date, its writer did not change, and nothing tests them together.\n\nRelation to prior entries. Entry 30 established that the deepest enforceable claim over the edge record is a degree floor, because the graph has one automated reader. This entry is the same shortage seen from the ranking side: the corpus is rich in relations that no scorer consumes, and the pairing relation is the one with a demonstrated cost attached. Recurrence, which pm-ouyq3n owns, is the other half — a defect family fixed thirty times is thirty unrelated nodes, and a contract broken at its seam is two healthy nodes. Both are invisible for the same reason, that relevance is currently computed per node.\n\nCaution against overfitting. This is one observation, not a fitted weight. The honest disposition is a candidate in the portfolio registry pm-g8t7b9 owns, scored against the judgment corpus before it is given any coefficient, and explicitly not promoted on the strength of the incident that suggested it."
  - created_at: "2026-08-08T22:00:52.713Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Context relevance ledger (32): epoch-conditioned evidence confidence — discount a record's recorded measurements by whether the contract that produced them is still the contract that would be applied to read them.\n\nRecorded 2026-08-08. Extends the ledger; entries 1 through 31 stand.\n\nThe observation this is derived from. A correct refusal blocked the daily cut because the event predated the tag producer the gate reads, so the gate could not judge it and failed closed. Generalized on the roadmap as horizon AH. The relevance consequence is separate from the gate consequence and is what this entry captures: when the engine assembles context, it currently treats every recorded measurement as equally citable, whether it was written under the contracts in force today or under contracts that have since been replaced. An agent handed a number extracted from a record written three epochs ago will reason about it as though it were current, and the record gives it no way to know otherwise.\n\nWhat the signal computes. For each candidate item, determine the contract epoch under which its recorded evidence was written — the schema version, error-code catalog version, dependency-kind lexicon, and projection contract in force at its write timestamps — and compare that to the epoch in force now. The signal is a scalar in the unit interval: one when the item's evidence was written entirely under the current epoch, decaying toward a floor as the number of intervening contract changes that touch fields the item actually carries increases. Weighting is by touched field, not by elapsed time: an item whose only recorded fields are ones no contract change has altered is not discounted at all, however old it is, and an item written last week under a since-replaced projection is.\n\nWhy it is a relevance signal and not a validity check. It never asserts that old evidence is wrong. Old evidence is usually right, and the immutable record is the reason the project can be trusted at all. It asserts only that old evidence is less safe to quote without re-derivation, which is exactly the judgement a ranking engine exists to make. In assembly it should surface as an explainable per-signal component, so a bounded context can say why a fresher but less central item outranked an older, more central one.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, mandatory as for every entry in this ledger. On a corpus with exactly one contract epoch — any newly initialized workspace, and any project that has never changed a contract — the signal is identically one for every item and the ranking is byte-identical to the ranking without it. On a two-item corpus it is likewise identically one. The signal can only ever discount, never promote, so it cannot manufacture confidence in an item and cannot raise any ceiling; the worst case it can produce is a ranking that is uniformly one epoch conservative. If epoch metadata is unavailable for an item the signal returns one rather than a penalty, so an unmeasurable item is never demoted for being unmeasurable.\n\nRelationship to neighbouring entries. Distinct from (26) recurrence-conditioned retrieval priors, which conditions on how often a family recurs; this conditions on whether the vocabulary the evidence is written in still applies. Distinct from (23) citation-stable evidence addressing, which asks whether a citation still resolves; this asks whether a citation that does resolve still means what it said. It composes with (31), contract-seam staleness: (31) ranks the pair whose two sides were last edited in different changes, and this entry ranks the record written before either edit.\n\nCandidate only. It requires an epoch representation that does not exist yet, which is horizon AH's terminal answer, so it must be routed through that work and must not be weighted on the strength of one incident. Reference evidence: pm-h75tjh."
  - created_at: "2026-08-09T06:06:13.120Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Context relevance ledger (33): coverage-ratio conditioning — a signal derived from a gate's verdict must be discounted by the fraction of the relevant population that gate actually examined, because a pass over a hand-picked sample and a pass over the whole tree carry different information and currently score identically.\n\nRecorded 2026-08-09. Extends the catalog; no prior entry is restarted or renumbered. Distinct from entry (32), epoch-conditioned evidence confidence, which discounts a measurement by whether the contract that produced it is still current. This entry discounts by breadth rather than by age: a verdict can be perfectly current and still cover a small, self-selected slice of what it names.\n\nGap this closes. The engine already treats gate verdicts and validation results as evidence when assembling context, and treats a passing gate as positive evidence about the surface it names. That inference is only sound when the gate examined the surface. Measured this pass: surface-replication-sets.json passes while covering zero of the five actual replication sites of the status-token normalization rule; smoke-external-packages.mjs would report ok over ten of eighteen published packages and runs in no workflow. Both are honest passes over unrepresentative samples, and an assembly that cites either as 'this surface is verified' is overstating what is known.\n\nMechanism. For each gate that can be cited as evidence, carry two numbers alongside the verdict: examined, and the population it claims to speak for. Where the population is computable — files matching the gate's own trigger globs, packages matching the discovery query, symbols in the declared surface — compute it; where it is not computable, mark the ratio unknown, which is itself the signal that the gate cannot be cited as coverage. Scale the evidence weight by that ratio, and surface the ratio in the served envelope next to the verdict so a reading agent can see the difference between verified and sampled.\n\nSecond-order use. The ratio is a work-selection signal in its own right. A gate with high citation frequency and a low or unknown coverage ratio is the highest-leverage thing to widen, because it is the point where the record is most confidently wrong. That ordering is computable from data the tracker already holds and needs no new judgement.\n\nRelation to prior entries. (30) priced signal admission by marginal information per unit of compute; this entry prices a signal already admitted, by how much of its claimed domain it observed. The two compose: coverage ratio is cheap to compute for any gate whose trigger set is declared, so it clears (30)'s exchange rate at essentially every corpus size."
  - created_at: "2026-08-09T13:39:47.942Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Context relevance ledger (34): property-lifetime conditioned evidence retention — the relevance of a recorded fact decays with the lifetime of the property it describes, never with the lifetime of the item that recorded it, and an assembler that treats a closed owner as evidence of staleness makes the same category error that retired a live guard in pm-5z9plz.\n\nRecorded 2026-08-09. Candidate only, routed through pm-g8t7b9 like every other entry in this ledger, and explicitly not weighted on the single incident that motivated it.\n\nThe signal. Every retrieval heuristic this project has considered treats an item's terminal status as a decay input, which is right for evidence about the work and wrong for evidence about the record. A closed item that measured a population, declared a bound, or established a corpus-wide invariant is describing something that outlives it: the population is still there, the bound is still meaningful, the invariant is still checkable. A closed item that describes its own progress, its own blockers, or its own review thread is describing something that ended when it ended. These are different retention classes and the corpus currently carries no marker separating them, so a single decay curve over closed_at necessarily over-retains the second and under-retains the first.\n\nThe proposal is a per-annotation retention class derived from what the annotation refers to rather than from who authored it. An annotation whose subject is a corpus-level property, detectable because it names a selector, a count over the whole record, a contract, or a gate, retains full weight after its owner terminates. An annotation whose subject is the item's own execution decays normally. The classifier is deliberately conservative: unclassifiable annotations take the existing decay, so the signal can only ever rescue evidence from premature decay and never accelerate it.\n\nWhy this is not entry (26) or (32). Entry (26) conditions retrieval on recurrence, which asks whether a defect came back; this asks whether a fact is still about anything. Entry (32) conditions confidence on the epoch a contract was emitted under, which asks whether a reader is competent to interpret a record; this asks whether the referent of the record still exists. A fact can be perfectly interpretable under the current epoch and describe a property that was deleted, and it can be uninterpretable and describe a property that is very much alive.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, mandatory as for every entry in this ledger. The term is identically neutral on a corpus with no terminal items, because there is nothing whose decay could be adjusted. It is identically neutral when no annotation classifies, which is the state of the corpus today, so shipping the classifier changes no ranking until classification exists. It is retention-only and may never raise an annotation's weight above what it carried while its owner was active, so the signal cannot be used to make a closed item outrank a live one. And on a corpus where every closed item's annotations classify as corpus-level, it degenerates to disabling terminal decay entirely, which must be a detectable and reportable state rather than a silent one, because that is the shape a misclassifier would produce.\n\nThe measurable claim, so this can be falsified rather than admired. Two thousand and thirty-six closed items hold the large majority of the record's annotations. If the classifier is real, a held-out set of questions answerable only from closed-item annotations should show a retrieval improvement concentrated entirely in the corpus-level class and no movement at all in the execution class. If the improvement is spread evenly, the classifier is reading authorship or age rather than reference, and the entry should be withdrawn."
  - created_at: "2026-08-09T20:05:21.715Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Context relevance ledger (35): subscriber-weighted item salience — an item is more relevant to an agent's working set the more declared predicates read it, because a change there propagates into enforcement instead of staying local, and the propagation is knowable from the record rather than guessed from the prose.\n\nRecorded 2026-08-09 evening, from the remote-effect measurement on pm-n7rr and horizon AK. The measurement showed that a mutating verb's real blast radius is not the files it writes but the set of external predicates over the record whose value it changes. Entry (34) established that the relevance of a fact decays with the lifetime of the property it describes rather than the lifetime of its owner. This entry is the retrieval-side consequence of the same observation applied to items rather than facts: an item that several declared predicates depend upon is load-bearing in a way no metadata field currently expresses, and an assembler that ranks it identically to an item nothing reads is discarding information the record already contains.\n\nThe signal. For each item, the count and kind of declared predicates whose evaluation reads it, weighted by the disposition of each predicate — an enforcing bound outranks a held one, and a held one outranks a retired one. The value is a boost applied to items already admitted by the retrieval stage, never an admission criterion of its own, because a subscriber count says how consequential a change would be and says nothing about whether the item answers the question being asked.\n\nDegeneracy clauses, mandatory as for every entry in this ledger. Identically uniform when the predicate registry is empty, which is the state today and will remain the state until horizon AK's registry exists, so the entry costs nothing before it can be computed. Identically uniform on a corpus where every item has the same subscriber count. Never raises a declared ceiling and never admits an item the retrieval stage rejected. Bounded by construction: the registry is repository data of registry size, not corpus size, so the computation is independent of item count and remains valid at a million items where a graph-centrality proxy for the same intuition would not.\n\nWhy it is not simply centrality, which is the objection this entry has to answer. Graph centrality measures how connected an item is to other items, and the typed graph is authored deliberately, so centrality partly measures how much attention an item has already received. Subscriber weight measures something the graph cannot see at all: coupling to mechanisms that live outside the record and are not nodes in it. The two are expected to correlate weakly and to disagree in the interesting cases, and the disagreement is the diagnostic — an item with high subscriber weight and low centrality is a silent coupling, which is precisely the failure horizon AK names.\n\nCandidate only, routed through pm-g8t7b9 like every other entry in this ledger, and explicitly not weighted on the single incident that motivated it. It is blocked in practice by the registry it reads, and it should not be implemented before there is a registry with more than a handful of rows, because a signal fitted to three subscribers would encode this session's accidents as a prior."
  - created_at: "2026-08-10T06:08:53.534Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (36), from pm-tld20c: the assembler is bounded by what the read surface will return, and the read surface currently withholds sections without saying so. An item can carry linked files, linked tests, and linked docs, and pm get returns none of them in any projection, including --full. There is no withheld-sections receipt, so the payload for an item with four linked files and two linked tests is byte-identical in those keys to the payload for an item with none.\n\nThis matters to the relevance engine specifically, not just to agent ergonomics. Linked files and linked tests are the highest-precision signal the record holds for the query an agent actually asks, which is which items concern the code I am about to change. Recurrence over linked-file paths is what identifies the wiring replication set; linked tests are what connect an item to executable verification. Scoring that ignores them is not scoring under a token budget, it is scoring under a truncated feature space, and no weighting over the remaining signals recovers the information.\n\nThe distinction worth preserving is between omitting a section deliberately to respect a budget and omitting it invisibly. The first is the engine working; the second makes the engine's own inputs unreliable, and worse, it is indistinguishable from a failed write, so it corrupts the operator's model of the record as well as the assembler's. A projection owes a receipt naming what it dropped and which command retrieves it, which is the same shape as the infeasible-intent receipt the token budget gate already verifies.\n\nSequencing note: this is upstream of further scoring work rather than parallel to it. Any measurement of relevance quality taken before the projection is fixed is taken over a feature set that silently excludes the two most precise signals available."
  - created_at: "2026-08-10T12:19:15.015Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Catalog entry (37), and unusually it is a constraint on the whole portfolio rather than a candidate signal: topology-derived features are mutable by governance passes, so any score that reads graph position is re-ranked by edits that change no item's content.\nRecorded 2026-08-10 from the strategic-ladder measurement. Three waves added 220 evidence-cited typed edges and nothing else — no title, description, body, tag, status or timestamp of any item changed in a way that alters what the item says. The effect on graph position was nevertheless large: items at ancestry depth 4 or deeper went from 460 to 1,036, the depth-3 population went from 833 to 1,250, rootless items went from 130 to 12, and the count of items reaching an outcome milestone went from 2,225 to 2,394.\nEvery one of those numbers is an input to a plausible relevance feature. Depth-to-root as a proxy for specificity, distance-to-outcome as a proxy for strategic weight, component size, centrality, eccentricity — all of them moved for more than 2,000 items in a single pass that added no information about any individual item. An engine deployed before that pass and unchanged after it would have returned materially different working sets for identical queries, and the cause would appear nowhere in any item's history because the cause is not in any item; it is in the shape of the set.\nThis is not an argument against topology features. It is a specification requirement that the portfolio has not stated, and it applies retroactively to several entries already in this ledger that read graph position.\nThe requirement, in three parts.\nFirst, a topology feature must be computed against a declared topology version, not against whatever the graph currently is. A governance pass that changes reachability is a re-scoring event and must be nameable as one. Without this, a relevance regression and a governance improvement are indistinguishable in the evaluation harness, and pm-atfm's graded judgments silently drift because their gold answers were fixed under an older topology.\nSecond, a topology feature must be normalized against the population it sits in rather than used as an absolute. Absolute depth 3 meant something different before and after this pass; depth relative to the corpus depth distribution meant close to the same thing. The absolute form encodes how much ladder-wiring work the tracker has received, which is a property of the project's governance history and not of the item.\nThird, the degeneracy clause every entry here carries needs an additional case: identically uniform under any pure re-parenting of the graph that preserves item content. A feature that fails this case is measuring curation effort, and curation effort correlates with attention already spent, so ranking on it is self-reinforcing — the items that have been organized most get retrieved most, which is the opposite of what a returning agent needs.\nSecondary observation from the same measurement, which is a candidate signal and is recorded as one rather than promoted here. The record stores adjacency twice: as typed edges, and as identifiers written into descriptions, comments, notes and learnings. 8,995 prose mentions resolve to real items and 1,750 distinct pairs exist only in prose, with no edge in either direction. The two copies differ systematically in time, because prose is written when an item is filed while its edges accrete over later passes or never arrive. Prose adjacency therefore has higher recall and lower precision than edge adjacency, and it is fresher. Merging them into one adjacency feature discards exactly that difference. They should be separate features with separate weights, and the prose feature must carry the exemption ledger from pm-ob9z4y so ledger surfaces such as the roadmap apex, which alone accounts for 405 of the 1,750, do not read as a dense hub. Candidate only, routed through pm-g8t7b9, and blocked in practice on the parity census existing."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-21T15:56:04.292Z",codex-tracker-governance,"Graph-aware context needs separate meanings for an actionable active isolate and a historical knowledge orphan. The 2026-07-21 graph had zero active isolates but many historical components or orphans; ranking should surface the former as governance risk while using the latter only when relevance, analogy, or diversity justifies their token cost."
